window.CHRONOS_ARTICLE_CONTENT={"1938-01-01-ind-001":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-001 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Creation of SNCF — Unification of France&#x27;s Main Railways</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-01-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The state received 51% of the new company&#x27;s capital.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 January 1938, in France, the milestone “Creation of SNCF — Unification of France&#x27;s Main Railways” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The state received 51% of the new company&#x27;s capital. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This transport / infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.groupe-sncf.com/en/group/history-archives/80-years-of-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-01-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-03-04-ind-002":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-002 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Dammam No. 7 Strikes Oil in Commercial Quantities</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-03-04 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The commercial discovery that launched Saudi Arabia&#x27;s oil era.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 4 March 1938, in Saudi Arabia, the milestone “Dammam No. 7 Strikes Oil in Commercial Quantities” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The commercial discovery that launched Saudi Arabia&#x27;s oil era. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This oil / extraction milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.aramco.com/en/news-media/elements-magazine/2025/the-desert-pioneer-behind-our-first-oil-discovery\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-03-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-03-12-000":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0000 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Anschluss of Austria</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-03-12 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Nazi Germany annexes Austria, accelerating the breakdown of the European security order.</p><p>The Austrian state lost its independence under pressure from the Nazi leadership, which had long demanded its incorporation into Germany. Austrian Nazis were also active inside the country, deepening the crisis.</p><p>On 12 March 1938, German troops crossed the border. The new regime rapidly dismantled political freedoms and began persecuting opponents of Nazism, including Austrian Jews. The annexation was given legal form on 13 March 1938.</p><p>The proclaimed Anschluss was not the result of negotiations between equal parties. The subsequent plebiscite took place only after Nazi control and repression had already been imposed.</p><p>Austria&#x27;s incorporation changed the balance of power in Central Europe and became another stage in the escalation of the Third Reich&#x27;s aggressive foreign policy.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-03-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-03-18-ind-003":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-003 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Mexico Nationalises the Oil Industry</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-03-18 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of the most significant nationalisations of natural resources in the twentieth century.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 18 March 1938, in Mexico, the milestone “Mexico Nationalises the Oil Industry” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: One of the most significant nationalisations of natural resources in the twentieth century. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This oil / economy milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gob.mx/agn/es/articulos/agnrecuerda-el-80-aniversario-de-la-expropiacion-petrolera?idiom=es\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-03-18\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-04-01-ind-004":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-004 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Nestlé Launches Nescafé</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-04-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Industrial instant-coffee technology reaches the mass market.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 April 1938, in Switzerland, the milestone “Nestlé Launches Nescafé” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Industrial instant-coffee technology reaches the mass market. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This food industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nestle.com/media/news/nescafe-coffee-celebrates-80-years\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-04-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-04-06-ind-005":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-005 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Discovery of Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-04-06 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A new polymer with exceptional chemical resistance and very low friction.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 6 April 1938, in United States, the milestone “Discovery of Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A new polymer with exceptional chemical resistance and very low friction. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This materials science / chemistry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://digital.sciencehistory.org/works/0g354g32t\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-04-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-05-23-ind-006":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-006 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Technical Design of the Uglich and Rybinsk Hydroelectric Complexes Approved</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-05-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Uglich HPP design capacity: 110 MW with two generating units.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 23 May 1938, in USSR, the milestone “Technical Design of the Uglich and Rybinsk Hydroelectric Complexes Approved” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Uglich HPP design capacity: 110 MW with two generating units. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This hydropower milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://uglich.ru/news/?newsid=2360\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-05-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-05-26-ind-007":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-007 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Foundation Stone Laid for Volkswagen&#x27;s Main Plant</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-05-26 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A project for one of the largest mass-production automobile factories of its time.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 26 May 1938, in Germany, the milestone “Foundation Stone Laid for Volkswagen&#x27;s Main Plant” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A project for one of the largest mass-production automobile factories of its time. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>This industrial history is inseparable from the Nazi war economy. The related article must also address coercion and forced labour where documented; the technical achievement is not presented as a value-neutral success.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.volkswagen-group.com/en/volkswagen-chronicle-17351/1937-to-1945-founding-of-the-company-and-integration-into-the-war-economy-17354\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-05-26\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-05-29-new-019":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-019 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>First Anti-Jewish Law in Hungary</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-05-29 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The law restricted Jewish participation in economic life.</p><p>The law restricted Jewish participation in economic life.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline-group/holocaust/1933-1938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-05-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-07-03-ind-008":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-008 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Mallard Sets the World Steam Locomotive Speed Record</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-07-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Maximum speed: approximately 203 km/h.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 3 July 1938, in United Kingdom, the milestone “Mallard Sets the World Steam Locomotive Speed Record” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Maximum speed: approximately 203 km/h. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This railway engineering milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://blog.railwaymuseum.org.uk/mallard-75-the-story-so-far/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-07-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-07-06-new-020":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-020 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Opening of the Évian Conference on Jewish Refugees</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-07-06 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Representatives of states discussed the refugee crisis created by persecution in Nazi-controlled territories.</p><p>Representatives of states discussed the refugee crisis created by persecution in Nazi-controlled territories.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline-event/holocaust/1933-1938/evian-conference\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-07-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-07-29-091":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0091 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battles at Lake Khasan</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-07-29 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Fighting broke out between the Red Army and forces of the Japanese Empire along the Soviet-Korean border.</p><p>A dispute over the heights near Lake Khasan escalated into a limited but intense armed conflict. Japanese units tried to consolidate positions that the Soviet side regarded as its territory.</p><p>The Soviet command brought in additional forces, including artillery and aircraft, and eventually restored control over the disputed area. The losses exposed weaknesses in training and command on both sides.</p><p>The conflict did not become a full-scale war, but it deepened tensions in East Asia. The following year, more serious fighting broke out at Khalkhin Gol.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_timelines_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-07-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-09-29-001":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0001 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Munich Agreement</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-09-29 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany, Italy, Britain and France agree to transfer the Sudetenland to Germany without Czechoslovakia taking part in the decisive negotiations.</p><p>After the Anschluss of Austria, Germany intensified its demands on Czechoslovakia over the Sudetenland. The region contained important fortifications and industrial facilities.</p><p>At the end of September, the leaders of Germany, Italy, Britain and France reached an agreement in Munich. Czechoslovakia was not a full participant in the decisive talks and was effectively confronted with an ultimatum. The negotiations ran from 29 to 30 September, and the agreement was signed during the night of 30 September 1938.</p><p>The withdrawal of Czechoslovak forces and the transfer of the Sudetenland weakened the country&#x27;s ability to defend itself and triggered a profound political crisis.</p><p>In March 1939, Germany occupied the Czech lands. The Munich Agreement therefore became a classic example of the failure of hopes that unilateral concessions to an aggressor would preserve peace.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-09-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-10-22-ind-009":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-009 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Chester Carlson Produces the First Xerographic Image</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-10-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The technological basis for future mass dry-copying of documents.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 22 October 1938, in United States, the milestone “Chester Carlson Produces the First Xerographic Image” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The technological basis for future mass dry-copying of documents. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This printing / office technology milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.si.edu/object/xerography-firstsite-senior-curator-michelle-cotton-assistant-curator-nicole-yip:siris_sil_1066740\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-10-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-10-30-worldnew001":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew001 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Orson Welles&#x27;s &#x27;The War of the Worlds&#x27; Radio Broadcast</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-10-30 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The radio drama demonstrated both the power and the risks of realistic audio journalism.</p><p>On 30 October 1938, the American CBS radio network broadcast an adaptation of H. G. Wells&#x27;s The War of the Worlds. Orson Welles and the Mercury Theatre on the Air used the format of news bulletins to tell a fictional story of a Martian invasion.</p><p>Listeners who tuned in after the beginning and missed the explanation that the programme was fictional sometimes interpreted individual reports as genuine. The broadcast demonstrated how persuasive fiction could sound when presented in an authoritative news format.</p><p>Later researchers questioned the popular story of nationwide mass panic: contemporary newspapers often exaggerated the scale of the reaction. Reports of anxiety among some listeners and calls to public institutions, however, were real.</p><p>The event became an important milestone in the history of broadcasting, media literacy and debates over editorial responsibility. For CHRONOS, it is also an example of radio&#x27;s social influence on the eve of the world war.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/infamous-war-worlds-radio-broadcast-was-magnificent-fluke-180955180/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-10-30\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-11-09-002":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0002 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Kristallnacht</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-11-09 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Mass anti-Jewish pogroms across Germany and annexed Austria on 9–10 November.</p><p>The Nazi regime had systematically restricted Jewish rights since 1933. The November pogroms of 1938 marked a transition to especially open and centrally directed violence.</p><p>During the night of 9–10 November and in the days that followed, synagogues, shops and homes were destroyed. People were humiliated, beaten, murdered and arrested in large numbers.</p><p>Thousands of Jewish men were imprisoned in concentration camps. State propaganda tried to portray the organised attacks as a spontaneous reaction by the population.</p><p>Kristallnacht became a turning point in the persecution of Europe&#x27;s Jews. Historians regard it as a major stage on the path to the Holocaust.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-11-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1938-12-02-new-022":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-022 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>First Kindertransport Arrives in Britain</h1><div class=\"meta\">1938-12-02 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Groups of child refugees from Nazi-controlled territories began arriving in Britain.</p><p>Groups of child refugees from Nazi-controlled territories began arriving in Britain.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kindertransport-1938-40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1938-12-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-01-01-ind-012":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-012 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Hewlett-Packard Founded</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-01-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The beginning of one of the key manufacturers of electronic measurement and computing equipment.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 January 1939, in United States, the milestone “Hewlett-Packard Founded” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The beginning of one of the key manufacturers of electronic measurement and computing equipment. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This electronics / instrumentation milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.hpe.com/us/en/about/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-01-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-03-15-003":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0003 · Western Europe</div><h1>Occupation of the Czech Lands</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-03-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany occupies Bohemia and Moravia after the breakup of Czechoslovakia.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-03-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-03-15-new-001":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-001 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Declaration of Independence of Carpatho-Ukraine</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-03-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Soim of Carpatho-Ukraine proclaimed independence in Khust.</p><p>The Soim of Carpatho-Ukraine proclaimed independence in Khust.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://uinp.gov.ua/istorychnyy-kalendar/berezen/15/1939-progolosheno-nezalezhnist-karpatskoyi-ukrayiny\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-03-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-03-31-new001":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0201 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>British and French Guarantees to Poland</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-03-31 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the expansion of German power, the British and French governments declared support for Polish independence. European diplomacy increasingly recognised the danger of another war.</p><p>After the expansion of German power, the British and French governments declared support for Polish independence. European diplomacy increasingly recognised the danger of another war.</p><p>On 31 March 1939, the British government announced a guarantee of Polish independence; French commitments to Poland were also part of the alliance system.</p><p>The declarations marked a change in London&#x27;s policy after earlier concessions to Germany. At the same time, the guarantees were not a plan for the immediate military defence of Polish territory.</p><p>After the German invasion in September 1939, Britain and France declared war on Germany. The March guarantees were an important diplomatic precondition for that decision.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-03-31\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-04-07-new002":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0202 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Italian Invasion of Albania</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-04-07 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Fascist Italy sought to strengthen its influence in the Balkans and control the strategic approaches to the Adriatic Sea. Albania was already under heavy Italian political and economic pressure.</p><p>Fascist Italy sought to strengthen its influence in the Balkans and control the strategic approaches to the Adriatic Sea. Albania was already under heavy Italian political and economic pressure.</p><p>On 7 April 1939, Italian forces began the invasion. Over the following days Albania was occupied and its state independence was effectively abolished.</p><p>The occupation occurred before Germany&#x27;s war against Poland. It formed part of the wider expansionist policy of Benito Mussolini&#x27;s regime.</p><p>The Italian presence in Albania later became important to fighting in the Balkans, especially Italy&#x27;s attack on Greece in October 1940.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-04-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-04-30-ind-014":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-014 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>RCA Demonstrates Electronic Television to the Public at the New York World&#x27;s Fair</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-04-30 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">An important step from experimental television toward a mass medium.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 30 April 1939, in United States, the milestone “RCA Demonstrates Electronic Television to the Public at the New York World&#x27;s Fair” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: An important step from experimental television toward a mass medium. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This electronics / communications milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.si.edu/object/david-sarnoff-1891-1971-seen-tv-viewers-1939-worlds-fair:siris_arc_367593\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-04-30\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-05-11-092":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0092 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Beginning of the Battles of Khalkhin Gol</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-05-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A series of clashes began on the border between Mongolia and Manchukuo involving Japanese and Soviet-Mongolian forces.</p><p>Border disputes developed into a campaign involving tanks, artillery and aircraft. Formally it remained a local conflict, but the participants committed substantial regular forces.</p><p>Fighting intensified through the summer, and in August troops under Georgy Zhukov launched an offensive that encircled the Japanese force.</p><p>Japan&#x27;s defeat influenced debate in Tokyo over the future direction of expansion. The war in Europe began before the fighting at Khalkhin Gol had fully ended.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1939)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-05-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-05-12-worldnew002":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew002 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Funding for the Harvard Mark I</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-05-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The automatic electromechanical calculator project received its initial funding.</p><p>On 12 May 1939, the first appropriation was granted for the future Harvard Mark I computing machine. The project was initiated by Harvard researcher Howard Aiken, who wanted to automate lengthy mathematical calculations.</p><p>Development was carried out with IBM and drew on experience with electromechanical machines, relays and punched cards. The project required a large number of components and careful synchronisation of mechanical and electrical assemblies.</p><p>The machine was not completed in 1939: the Harvard Mark I was formally presented to the university in August 1944. This entry therefore concerns the start of funding, not the computer&#x27;s entry into service.</p><p>The story of the Mark I shows how investment in automated calculation before the war contributed to the development of computing. Such machines later became part of the broader transition from mechanical calculators to programmable systems.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computerhistory.org/tdih/may/12/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-05-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-05-13-new-023":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-023 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>The MS St. Louis Sails from Hamburg</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-05-13 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Jewish refugees attempted to find safety across the Atlantic.</p><p>Jewish refugees attempted to find safety across the Atlantic.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline-event/holocaust/1939-1941/st-louis-voyage\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-05-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-06-28-ind-015":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-015 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Regular Transatlantic Passenger Flight by Pan American</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-06-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">22 passengers and 11 crew aboard the Boeing 314 Dixie Clipper.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 28 June 1939, in United States / Atlantic / Europe, the milestone “First Regular Transatlantic Passenger Flight by Pan American” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: 22 passengers and 11 crew aboard the Boeing 314 Dixie Clipper. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This civil aviation milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://airandspace.si.edu/stories/editorial/passenger-number-one-pan-american-first-transatlantic-flight\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-06-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-08-23-004":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0004 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-08-23 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union sign a non-aggression pact with a secret protocol dividing spheres of influence.</p><p>In the summer of 1939, European security negotiations were failing while Berlin prepared to attack Poland. Moscow was simultaneously conducting diplomatic contacts with several sides.</p><p>On 23 August, Germany and the Soviet Union signed a non-aggression treaty. It was accompanied by a secret protocol defining spheres of influence in Eastern Europe.</p><p>The secret arrangements created the conditions for the partition of Polish territories after the German invasion in September. They also affected the fate of the Baltic states.</p><p>The pact was not a neutral agreement on peaceful coexistence: its secret provisions became an instrument of territorial division and the loss of sovereignty by neighbouring countries.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-08-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","world-1939-08-27-00":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №1000 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Jet-Powered Heinkel He 178</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-08-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The German experimental Heinkel He 178 made its first flight using turbojet propulsion, an important early demonstration of a powerplant principle that would transform aviation.</p><p>The German experimental Heinkel He 178 made its first flight using turbojet propulsion. It was an important early demonstration of a new propulsion principle that would later transform aviation.</p><p>The event belongs to the history of technology rather than to the outcome of a particular battle. It should be distinguished from the later mass deployment of jet aircraft during the war.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinkel_He_178\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-08-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-09-01-005":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0005 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>German Invasion of Poland</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-09-01 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The beginning of the Second World War in Europe; Germany attacks from the west.</p><p>After months of diplomatic crisis, Germany launched a large-scale invasion of Poland on 1 September 1939. The German command sought the rapid defeat of the Polish armed forces.</p><p>German forces advanced from several directions with support from aircraft and armoured formations. The Polish army resisted, but its position deteriorated because of the attacker&#x27;s advantages in mobility and fire support.</p><p>On 3 September, Britain and France declared war on Germany. On 17 September, the Red Army invaded from the east in accordance with Soviet-German arrangements.</p><p>The invasion marked the beginning of the Second World War in Europe. The occupation of Poland brought mass repression, forced labour, terror against civilians and persecution of Jews.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-09-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-09-01-ind-016":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-016 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Full-Scale Polyethylene Plant Begins Operation</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-09-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Polyethylene moves from laboratory material to industrial production.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 September 1939, in United Kingdom, the milestone “First Full-Scale Polyethylene Plant Begins Operation” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Polyethylene moves from laboratory material to industrial production. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This chemical industry / plastics milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co9978/ethylene-compressor-from-first-full-scale-polyethe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-09-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-09-03-006":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0006 · Western Europe</div><h1>Britain and France Declare War</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-09-03 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The two states enter the war against Germany following the invasion of Poland.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-09-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-09-14-ind-018":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-018 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Sikorsky VS-300 Helicopter</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-09-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A key prototype for the modern single-main-rotor helicopter configuration.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 14 September 1939, in United States, the milestone “First Flight of the Sikorsky VS-300 Helicopter” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A key prototype for the modern single-main-rotor helicopter configuration. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://sikorskyarchives.com/vs-300-helicopter-design/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-09-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-09-17-007":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0007 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Soviet Invasion of Poland</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-09-17 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Red Army enters eastern Poland in accordance with the Soviet-German division of spheres of influence.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-09-17\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-09-28-new003":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0203 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Surrender of Warsaw</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-09-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After Germany&#x27;s attack on Poland, Warsaw came under siege. The city&#x27;s defenders and civilian population endured heavy artillery fire and bombing.</p><p>After Germany&#x27;s attack on Poland, Warsaw came under siege. The city&#x27;s defenders and civilian population endured heavy artillery fire and bombing.</p><p>At the end of September 1939, organised Polish defence of the capital ceased. German troops took control of the city following its surrender.</p><p>The fall of the capital was one of the decisive episodes of the Polish campaign, but it did not mean the end of Polish resistance or of Polish state institutions operating in exile.</p><p>The occupation of Warsaw began a long period of Nazi terror, persecution of the city&#x27;s inhabitants and destruction of its Jewish population.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-09-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-11-30-008":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0008 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Winter War</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-11-30 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Soviet Union attacks Finland; fighting continues until March 1940.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-11-30\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-12-12-ind-019":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-019 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>World&#x27;s First Industrial Nylon Plant Begins Operation</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-12-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">In its first year, it produced enough nylon for about 64 million pairs of stockings.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 12 December 1939, in United States, the milestone “World&#x27;s First Industrial Nylon Plant Begins Operation” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: In its first year, it produced enough nylon for about 64 million pairs of stockings. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This chemical industry / textiles milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://archives.delaware.gov/delaware-historical-markers/worlds-first-nylon-plant/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-12-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1939-12-13-093":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0093 · Atlantic and Naval War</div><h1>Battle of the River Plate</h1><div class=\"meta\">1939-12-13 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">British cruisers engaged the German commerce raider Admiral Graf Spee off the coast of South America.</p><p>The German ship had been attacking Allied merchant shipping in the Atlantic. Three British cruisers intercepted it near the River Plate estuary and damaged it in an artillery engagement.</p><p>Admiral Graf Spee entered neutral Montevideo. Under diplomatic pressure and uncertainty about the enemy forces outside the port, the commander decided to scuttle the ship off the harbour entrance.</p><p>The event became an early symbol of the global character of the naval war: operations by European powers were taking place far from Europe itself.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1939)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1939-12-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-02-21-ind-021":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-021 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>High-Power Cavity Magnetron Successfully Tested in Birmingham</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-02-21 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The technological foundation for compact microwave radar.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 21 February 1940, in United Kingdom, the milestone “High-Power Cavity Magnetron Successfully Tested in Birmingham” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The technological foundation for compact microwave radar. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This radar / electronics milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/Documents/culture/4812BluePlaquebrochureAW.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-02-21\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-02-23-ind-022":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-022 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Russell Ohl Observes the p–n Junction in Silicon</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-02-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A fundamental milestone for the future semiconductor industry.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 23 February 1940, in United States, the milestone “Russell Ohl Observes the p–n Junction in Silicon” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A fundamental milestone for the future semiconductor industry. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This semiconductors milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/discovery-of-the-p-n-junction/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-02-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-03-12-094":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0094 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Moscow Peace Treaty with Finland</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-03-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Winter War ends with a peace treaty under which Finland cedes territory to the Soviet Union.</p><p>Despite the Red Army&#x27;s far greater resources, Finnish defence inflicted serious losses on Soviet forces. Sustained pressure and the eventual breach of defensive positions nevertheless forced Helsinki to accept negotiations.</p><p>The treaty transferred the Karelian Isthmus, including Vyborg, and other territories to the Soviet Union. Hundreds of thousands of inhabitants left the ceded areas and were resettled elsewhere in Finland.</p><p>Finland preserved its independence, but the conflict left a deep national trauma. It also influenced international assessments of the Red Army&#x27;s combat effectiveness.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1940)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-03-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-04-09-009":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0009 · Western Europe</div><h1>Invasion of Denmark and Norway</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-04-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany begins an operation to occupy two Scandinavian states.</p><p>Norway and Denmark sought to remain neutral, but their strategic position was critical for control of the North Sea and routes supplying raw materials.</p><p>On 9 April 1940, German forces invaded both countries. The Danish government quickly ended organised resistance, while the Norwegian army continued fighting with Allied support.</p><p>Combat took place in ports, fjords and mountainous areas. British and French forces mounted amphibious operations, but were unable to hold the country.</p><p>The Norwegian government and king went into exile. German control of the coastline affected the naval and air war in the North Atlantic.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-04-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-04-11-131":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0131 · Atlantic and Naval War</div><h1>First Naval Battle of Narvik</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-04-10 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">British destroyers attacked German ships in Narvik harbour.</p><p>In early April 1940, Germany invaded Norway in an effort to control important ports and sea routes. Narvik was especially important because Swedish iron ore was shipped through the port during the winter season.</p><p>On 10 April, British destroyers launched a surprise attack on the German force around Narvik. The fighting took place among narrow fjords, where ships manoeuvred under fire at short ranges.</p><p>Both sides suffered losses; British ships attacked German warships and transports but were themselves hit. The engagement did not end the Norwegian campaign.</p><p>A second battle took place near Narvik two days later. These operations demonstrated how strongly control of the sea shaped the land campaign in northern Europe.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1940)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-04-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-04-13-132":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0132 · Atlantic and Naval War</div><h1>Second Naval Battle of Narvik</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-04-13 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A British force again attacked German destroyers in the Norwegian fjords.</p><p>After the first engagement, German destroyers remained at Narvik with limited fuel and ammunition. The Allies sought to deprive the German landing force of support from the sea.</p><p>On 13 April, the British battleship Warspite and destroyers entered the fjords. Supporting aircraft and naval gunfire helped destroy or disable the remaining German destroyers.</p><p>German ground troops still held positions around Narvik. Victory at sea did not bring the immediate liberation of the port.</p><p>The battle&#x27;s significance lay in Germany&#x27;s loss of a substantial share of its destroyer force and in the demonstration of the Royal Navy&#x27;s role in the Norwegian campaign.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1940)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-04-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-04-14-133":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0133 · Western Europe</div><h1>Allied Landing at Namsos</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-04-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">British and French forces began landing in central Norway.</p><p>After the German invasion, the Allies planned to support Norway and regain the initiative around Trondheim. Sea transport was critical because overland communications were poor.</p><p>On 14 April, British and French troops began landing in the Namsos area. The Allies hoped to advance toward Trondheim together with Norwegian units.</p><p>Difficult supply conditions and German air superiority made the bridgeheads increasingly hard to maintain. The attempt to alter the situation in central Norway did not produce the expected result.</p><p>By the end of April, the Allies had begun withdrawing, and in early May a large part of the force was evacuated from the area.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1940)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-04-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-04-27-134":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0134 · Western Europe</div><h1>Allied Withdrawal from Central Norway</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-04-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">British command begins withdrawing troops from the Trondheim area.</p><p>The Norwegian campaign developed faster than the Allies could deploy reinforcements. Germany used captured airfields, while British and French forces remained dependent on supply by sea.</p><p>On 27 April, withdrawal began from positions north and south of Trondheim. The evacuation took place under the threat of air attack.</p><p>The abandonment of central Norway did not end the campaign: Allied operations continued around Narvik for several more weeks.</p><p>The failure triggered sharp political debate in Britain and highlighted problems of coordination within the Allied command.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1940)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-04-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-04-30-new-025":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-025 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Sealing of the Łódź Ghetto</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-04-30 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The German administration isolated the city&#x27;s Jewish population.</p><p>The German administration isolated the city&#x27;s Jewish population.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lodz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-04-30\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-05-10-010":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0010 · Western Europe</div><h1>German Offensive in France and the Benelux</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-05-10 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">German forces attack the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg and France.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-05-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-05-14-new004":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0204 · Western Europe</div><h1>Capitulation of the Netherlands</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-05-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">On 10 May 1940, German forces launched an offensive against the Netherlands as part of the wider attack on Western Europe.</p><p>On 10 May 1940, German forces launched an offensive against the Netherlands as part of the wider attack on Western Europe.</p><p>German forces advanced rapidly across the country. The bombing of Rotterdam became one of the best-known tragedies of the campaign.</p><p>On 14 May, the Netherlands agreed to the surrender of its main forces, although isolated fighting continued. A period of German occupation began.</p><p>The fall of the Netherlands accelerated the strategic collapse in Western Europe and increased the danger to neighbouring Belgium and France.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-05-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-05-15-ind-023":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-023 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Nylon Stockings Go on Mass Sale</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-05-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Synthetic fibres enter the mass consumer market.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 15 May 1940, in United States, the milestone “Nylon Stockings Go on Mass Sale” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Synthetic fibres enter the mass consumer market. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This chemical industry / consumer goods milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_1200996\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-05-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-05-26-011":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0011 · Western Europe</div><h1>Evacuation from Dunkirk</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-05-26 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Allies begin evacuating a large number of troops across the English Channel.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-05-26\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-05-28-135":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0135 · Western Europe</div><h1>Allies Recapture Narvik</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-05-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Norwegian and Allied forces regain control of Narvik.</p><p>After the German invasion of Norway, Narvik remained one of the principal points of contention. Its strategic importance was linked to the export of Swedish iron ore.</p><p>At the end of May, Norwegian units supported by French, Polish and British forces went on the offensive. On 28 May, the Allies regained control of the town.</p><p>Despite the local success, the defeat of France forced the Allied command to reconsider its priorities. Evacuation from Norway began soon afterwards.</p><p>The capture of Narvik was an important symbolic Allied success, but it did not lead to the long-term retention of a northern bridgehead.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Norwegian_campaign\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-05-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-06-10-012":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0012 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Italy Enters the War</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-06-10 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Mussolini&#x27;s regime declares war on France and Britain.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-06-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-06-14-095":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0095 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Soviet Ultimatum to Lithuania</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-06-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Soviet Union presented Lithuania with an ultimatum that was followed by Soviet occupation of the country.</p><p>After the 1939 pact, the Baltic states came under increasing pressure from Moscow. The Soviet government demanded the formation of a new government and the admission of additional Soviet troops.</p><p>Lithuania accepted the ultimatum; on 15 June Soviet troops occupied strategic positions. A similar scenario was soon imposed on Latvia and Estonia.</p><p>Behind the formal changes to political institutions lay forced incorporation into the Soviet Union, which much of the Western world did not recognise as lawful.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1940)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-06-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-06-22-013":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0013 · Western Europe</div><h1>French Armistice with Germany</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-06-22 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After France&#x27;s defeat, an armistice is signed; a large part of the country comes under occupation.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-06-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-06-28-new005":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0205 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Romania Agrees to Cede Bessarabia and Northern Bukovina</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-06-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">In June 1940, the Soviet Union issued Romania an ultimatum demanding Bessarabia and the northern part of Bukovina.</p><p>In June 1940, the Soviet Union issued Romania an ultimatum demanding Bessarabia and the northern part of Bukovina.</p><p>On 28 June, Soviet forces began entering the territories after the Romanian government agreed under ultimatum pressure to withdraw.</p><p>The border changes affected large numbers of local inhabitants, including Ukrainians, Romanians, Jews and other communities.</p><p>The incorporation of these territories into the Soviet Union became an important part of the remaking of Eastern Europe&#x27;s political map on the eve of the German-Soviet war.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-06-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-07-10-014":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0014 · Air War</div><h1>Battle of Britain</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-07-10 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Luftwaffe begins a major air campaign against Britain.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-07-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-08-27-ind-026":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-026 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Caproni Campini N.1 Experimental Jet Aircraft</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-08-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of the earliest full-scale demonstrations of jet propulsion.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 27 August 1940, in Italy, the milestone “First Flight of the Caproni Campini N.1 Experimental Jet Aircraft” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: One of the earliest full-scale demonstrations of jet propulsion. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / jet technology milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dafhistory.af.mil/Portals/16/documents/Studies/1-50/AFD-090602-046.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-08-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-08-29-ind-027":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-027 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Marl Plant Produces Its First Bales of Buna Synthetic Rubber</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-08-29 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Initial annual capacity: approximately 18,000 t.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 29 August 1940, in Germany, the milestone “Marl Plant Produces Its First Bales of Buna Synthetic Rubber” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Initial annual capacity: approximately 18,000 t. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This chemical industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://history.evonik.com/en/locations/marl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-08-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-09-07-015":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0015 · Air War</div><h1>Beginning of the London Blitz</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-09-07 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">German aircraft begin a series of mass raids on London.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-09-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-09-11-ind-028":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-028 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Bell Labs Publicly Demonstrates Remote Computing over Telephone Lines</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-09-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">An early demonstration of remote access to a computing machine.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 11 September 1940, in United States, the milestone “Bell Labs Publicly Demonstrates Remote Computing over Telephone Lines” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: An early demonstration of remote access to a computing machine. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This computing / telecommunications milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computerhistory.org/tdih/september/11/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-09-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-09-23-ind-029":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-029 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>American Bantam Delivers the First Prototype of the Future Jeep</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-09-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">49 days elapsed from order to prototype delivery.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 23 September 1940, in United States, the milestone “American Bantam Delivers the First Prototype of the Future Jeep” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: 49 days elapsed from order to prototype delivery. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://bantam.us/heritage/the-original-jeep/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-09-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-09-27-016":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0016 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Tripartite Pact</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-09-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany, Italy and Japan formalise a military-political alliance.</p><p>Berlin, Rome and Tokyo formalised a political and military alliance intended to deter potential opponents. Their interests did not always coincide, however, and they often conducted military operations independently.</p><p>The Axis powers sought to coordinate their international positions and warn potential adversaries against intervention. The document formalised the political link between the European aggressor states and Japan.</p><p>The pact provided for mutual support in certain circumstances but did not create a unified operational command. The strategic objectives of its members often diverged.</p><p>The agreement intensified international confrontation on the eve of the further expansion of the war in the Mediterranean and the Pacific.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-09-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-10-28-017":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0017 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Italian Invasion of Greece</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-10-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Italian forces invade Greece from occupied Albania.</p><p>Italian forces advanced from occupied Albania, but the Greek army checked them and launched a counteroffensive. Later German intervention radically changed the situation in the Balkans.</p><p>Rome planned a quick victory and an expansion of its control in the Balkans. Italian forces instead encountered determined resistance in difficult mountainous terrain.</p><p>The Greek army not only stopped the advance but counterattacked into Albania. The Italian campaign became bogged down amid winter conditions.</p><p>The failure forced Berlin to pay greater attention to the Balkans. In the spring of 1941, Germany intervened in the war against Greece and Yugoslavia.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-10-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1940-11-15-018":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0018 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Warsaw Ghetto Sealed</h1><div class=\"meta\">1940-11-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The German occupation authorities isolate Jews inside the Warsaw ghetto.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline/holocaust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1940-11-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-01-14-ind-031":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-031 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Construction Begins on Kaiser&#x27;s First Richmond Shipyard</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-01-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The start of one of the major US centres of assembly-line shipbuilding.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 14 January 1941, in United States, the milestone “Construction Begins on Kaiser&#x27;s First Richmond Shipyard” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The start of one of the major US centres of assembly-line shipbuilding. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This shipbuilding milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/rori/learn/historyculture/historic-richmond-shipyards.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-01-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-02-12-019":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0019 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Rommel Arrives in North Africa</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-02-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">German forces begin deploying to support Italy in North Africa.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-02-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-03-01-new006":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0206 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Bulgaria Joins the Tripartite Pact</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-03-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">In early 1941, diplomatic and military pressure by the Axis powers in the Balkans was increasing. The region was strategically important for future operations against Greece and Yugoslavia.</p><p>In early 1941, diplomatic and military pressure by the Axis powers in the Balkans was increasing. The region was strategically important for future operations against Greece and Yugoslavia.</p><p>On 1 March 1941, Bulgaria joined the Tripartite Pact, formally aligning itself with the Axis powers.</p><p>The decision facilitated the movement of German troops into south-eastern Europe. Bulgaria&#x27;s participation in particular military operations nevertheless had its own limits.</p><p>The decision altered the balance of forces in the Balkans immediately before the German attacks on Yugoslavia and Greece.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-03-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-03-11-020":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0020 · Atlantic and Naval War</div><h1>Lend-Lease Act</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-03-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The United States adopts legislation authorising the supply of military materials to Allied countries.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-03-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-04-06-021":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0021 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Invasion of Yugoslavia and Greece</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-04-06 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany and its allies begin the Balkan campaign.</p><p>The German offensive rapidly shattered Yugoslavia&#x27;s defences and forced Greek forces to retreat. The occupation of the Balkans led to prolonged partisan warfare, repression and heavy civilian losses.</p><p>After a political crisis in Yugoslavia, Berlin attacked the country while simultaneously launching an offensive against Greece. German aircraft carried out destructive raids, including attacks on Belgrade.</p><p>A rapid armoured campaign overwhelmed organised Yugoslav resistance, while Axis forces in Greece broke through Allied defences. Part of the British force was evacuated by sea.</p><p>The occupation of the Balkans produced years of armed resistance, repression and mass violence against civilians. The Balkan theatre remained important until the end of the war.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-04-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-04-09-ind-032":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-032 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Companhia Siderúrgica Nacional (CSN) Established</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-04-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The foundation for large-scale integrated steel production in Brazil.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 9 April 1941, in Brazil, the milestone “Companhia Siderúrgica Nacional (CSN) Established” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The foundation for large-scale integrated steel production in Brazil. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This metallurgy milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.csn.com.br/quem-somos/historico/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-04-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-04-10-new007":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0207 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Proclamation of the Independent State of Croatia</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-04-10 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the Axis invasion of Yugoslavia began, the country&#x27;s political and military system collapsed rapidly.</p><p>After the Axis invasion of Yugoslavia began, the country&#x27;s political and military system collapsed rapidly.</p><p>On 10 April 1941, leaders of the Ustaše movement proclaimed the so-called Independent State of Croatia. Germany and Italy immediately recognised it.</p><p>The new regime also included Bosnia and Herzegovina and pursued policies of violence and persecution against Serbs, Jews, Roma and political opponents.</p><p>The proclamation formed part of the dismemberment of Yugoslavia and created conditions for large-scale crimes and prolonged armed resistance.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-04-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-04-24-ind-033":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-033 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Detroit Tank Arsenal Produces Its First Production Tank</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-04-24 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Automotive mass-production methods are transferred to heavy armoured vehicles.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 24 April 1941, in United States, the milestone “Detroit Tank Arsenal Produces Its First Production Tank” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Automotive mass-production methods are transferred to heavy armoured vehicles. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This mass manufacturing milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://asc.army.mil/docs/pubs/alt/archives/1994/Sep-Oct_1994.PDF\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-04-24\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","world-1941-05-12-01":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №1001 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Programmable Z3 Computer Operates</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-05-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Konrad Zuse demonstrated the Z3, a programmable electromechanical computing machine. It used relays and read its program from punched film.</p><p>Konrad Zuse demonstrated the Z3, a programmable electromechanical computing machine. It used relays and read its program from punched film.</p><p>The Z3 occupies a distinct place in computing history as an early working programmable digital computer. It should not be confused with later fully electronic machines.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computinghistory.org.uk/cgi/computing-timeline.pl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-05-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-05-15-ind-034":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-034 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of Britain&#x27;s Gloster E.28/39 Jet Aircraft</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-05-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The first flight lasted 17 minutes; a key milestone for Frank Whittle&#x27;s jet engine.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 15 May 1941, in United Kingdom, the milestone “First Flight of Britain&#x27;s Gloster E.28/39 Jet Aircraft” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The first flight lasted 17 minutes; a key milestone for Frank Whittle&#x27;s jet engine. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / jet engines milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/research-enquiries/history-of-aviation-timeline/british-military-aviation/1941-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-05-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-05-20-022":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0022 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Battle of Crete</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-05-20 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">German airborne forces begin an operation to seize the island.</p><p>The airborne operation enabled German forces to capture the island, although the paratroops suffered exceptionally heavy losses. The battle demonstrated both the possibilities and the dangers of large-scale airborne assaults.</p><p>Paratroopers and glider troops landed under intense fire from British, Greek and New Zealand forces. The first days inflicted severe losses on the German airborne units.</p><p>The capture of Maleme airfield allowed the Germans to fly in reinforcements and turn the campaign in their favour. Allied forces began evacuating the island.</p><p>The victory gave the Axis an important position in the eastern Mediterranean, but the high losses influenced Germany&#x27;s later use of major airborne operations.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-05-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-05-24-023":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0023 · Atlantic and Naval War</div><h1>Battle of the Denmark Strait</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-05-24 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The German battleship Bismarck sinks the British battlecruiser HMS Hood.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-05-24\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-06-22-024":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0024 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Operation Barbarossa</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-06-22 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Nazi Germany and its allies attack the Soviet Union, opening the largest land front of the war.</p><p>Germany prepared the attack on the Soviet Union for many months. The plan called for the rapid destruction of Soviet forces and the occupation of vast territories.</p><p>On 22 June 1941, German forces and allied armies crossed the Soviet border. The offensive began simultaneously on northern, central and southern axes.</p><p>During the first weeks, the Red Army suffered severe defeats, retreated over great distances and lost large formations in encirclements. The German advance, however, gradually encountered growing logistical and operational difficulties.</p><p>The invasion opened a war of annihilation accompanied by mass crimes against civilians and prisoners of war, the Holocaust and catastrophic human losses.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-06-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-07-06-new-026":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-026 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Mass Murder of Jews at Kaunas Seventh Fort</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-07-06 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of the early large-scale mass shootings following the invasion of the Soviet Union.</p><p>One of the early large-scale mass shootings following the invasion of the Soviet Union.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-07-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-07-11-ind-035":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-035 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>State Defense Committee Launches Large-Scale Evacuation of Soviet Industry Eastward</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-07-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">According to consolidated historical data, 1,523 large enterprises were evacuated between July and November.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 11 July 1941, in USSR, the milestone “State Defense Committee Launches Large-Scale Evacuation of Soviet Industry Eastward” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: According to consolidated historical data, 1,523 large enterprises were evacuated between July and November. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This industrial logistics / relocation milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://docs.historyrussia.org/ru/nodes/300220\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-07-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-07-12-100":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0100 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Anglo-Soviet Agreement on Joint Action</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-07-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Britain and the Soviet Union agree to cooperate against Nazi Germany.</p><p>Germany&#x27;s attack on the Soviet Union radically changed the diplomatic situation. States that had previously regarded one another with hostility now shared a common military enemy.</p><p>The agreement provided for mutual assistance and a pledge not to make a separate peace with Berlin. It was one of the first steps toward the broader anti-Hitler coalition.</p><p>Differences over the future political order of Europe did not disappear, but the need to coordinate the war effort took precedence until the fighting ended.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1941)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-07-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-07-15-ind-036":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-036 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Willys-Overland Receives Contract for Mass Production of the Military Jeep</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-07-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Jeep design is standardised for mass production.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 15 July 1941, in United States, the milestone “Willys-Overland Receives Contract for Mass Production of the Military Jeep” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The Jeep design is standardised for mass production. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://o.media.stellantis.com/uk-en/jeep/press/jeep-celebrates-80-years-by-building-an-electric-present-and-future\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-07-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-08-03-new-027":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-027 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Bishop von Galen&#x27;s Sermon against the Murder of Patients</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-08-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Catholic bishop publicly condemned the Nazi &#x27;euthanasia&#x27; programme.</p><p>The Catholic bishop publicly condemned the Nazi &#x27;euthanasia&#x27; programme.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-08-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-08-14-new-112":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-112 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Atlantic Charter</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-08-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Roosevelt and Churchill published principles for the post-war international order.</p><p>Roosevelt and Churchill published principles for the post-war international order.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/atlantic-conf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-08-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-09-08-025":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0025 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Beginning of the Siege of Leningrad</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-09-08 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Land routes to Leningrad are cut; the siege will cause mass civilian death.</p><p>After the land communications were cut, the city entered a prolonged siege that caused famine and enormous loss of life. Supply across Lake Ladoga sustained part of the population but could not end the humanitarian catastrophe.</p><p>The advance of Army Group North and Finnish operations placed the city of millions under grave threat. Once the last land connection was lost, supply became extraordinarily difficult.</p><p>The city endured mass starvation, shelling and extreme cold. Supply over Lake Ladoga partly eased conditions but could not remove the humanitarian disaster.</p><p>The siege lasted more than two years and killed a vast number of civilians. It was finally lifted in January 1944.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-09-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-09-19-new-008":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-008 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>German Forces Capture Kyiv</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-09-19 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The capital of the Ukrainian SSR falls under German occupation.</p><p>The capital of the Ukrainian SSR falls under German occupation.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline-event/holocaust/1939-1941/occupation-of-kiev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-09-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-09-27-ind-039":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-039 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Liberty Ship — SS Patrick Henry — Launched</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-09-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Typical series length: about 134.6 m; deadweight about 10,800 t; more than 2,700 ships built during the war.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 27 September 1941, in United States, the milestone “First Liberty Ship — SS Patrick Henry — Launched” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Typical series length: about 134.6 m; deadweight about 10,800 t; more than 2,700 ships built during the war. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This shipbuilding milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://home.nps.gov/articles/liberty-ships-and-victory-ships-america-s-lifeline-in-war-teaching-with-historic-places.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-09-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-09-29-026":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0026 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Babyn Yar in Kyiv</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-09-29 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">On 29–30 September, Nazi occupiers shoot tens of thousands of Kyiv&#x27;s Jews.</p><p>After the capture of Kyiv in September 1941, the Nazi occupiers began the mass murder of the city&#x27;s Jewish population. Jews were ordered to assemble at a designated place while the true purpose was concealed.</p><p>On 29–30 September, Einsatzgruppen units with the participation of other German formations shot tens of thousands of Jews at Babyn Yar. The perpetrators&#x27; own records give the figure of 33,771 victims for those two days.</p><p>During the occupation, Roma, Soviet prisoners of war and other people persecuted by the Nazi authorities were also murdered at Babyn Yar.</p><p>Babyn Yar is one of the best-known sites of the Holocaust by bullets. The history of how this crime was remembered is important for understanding both the war and post-war memory politics.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-09-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-10-02-027":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0027 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Operation Typhoon</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-10-02 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The German offensive toward Moscow enters its decisive phase.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-10-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-10-04-ind-040":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-040 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Grand Coulee Generator Begins Operation</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-10-04 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Rated output of the first generator: 108 MW.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 4 October 1941, in United States, the milestone “First Grand Coulee Generator Begins Operation” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Rated output of the first generator: 108 MW. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This hydropower milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/washington-grand-coulee-dam.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-10-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-11-18-102":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0102 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Operation Crusader</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-11-18 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The British Eighth Army goes on the offensive in North Africa.</p><p>The British command sought to relieve Tobruk and push Axis forces away from the Egyptian frontier. Fighting in the desert was marked by rapid armoured manoeuvres.</p><p>The operation involved heavy tank losses on both sides. After prolonged fighting, Rommel&#x27;s forces withdrew and the siege of Tobruk was lifted.</p><p>The success did not end the campaign: the front in Libya shifted repeatedly before the final defeat of Axis forces in 1943.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1941)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-11-18\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-12-05-028":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0028 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Soviet Counteroffensive at Moscow</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-12-05 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces begin a large-scale counteroffensive around Moscow.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-12-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-12-07-029":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0029 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Attack on Pearl Harbor</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-12-07 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Japan strikes the US naval base in Hawaii.</p><p>By December 1941, relations between the United States and Japan had deteriorated sharply because of Japanese expansion in Asia and economic sanctions. Japan&#x27;s leadership decided to launch a preventive strike against the US Pacific Fleet.</p><p>On 7 December, Japanese carrier aircraft attacked the Pearl Harbor base in Hawaii. Many ships were sunk or damaged, aircraft were destroyed, and thousands of American servicemen and civilians were killed.</p><p>The American aircraft carriers were not in the harbour at the time of the attack. The strike did not destroy the United States&#x27; ability to wage a prolonged war in the Pacific.</p><p>The next day, the United States declared war on Japan. The attack became a turning point that brought the United States directly into the global conflict.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-12-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-12-08-030":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0030 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>United States Enters the War against Japan</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-12-08 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The US Congress declares war on Japan as the Japanese offensive continues across the Pacific.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-12-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-12-08-new-028":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-028 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Mass Killing Begins at Chełmno</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-12-08 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The killing centre began using gas vans.</p><p>The killing centre began using gas vans.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline/holocaust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-12-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1941-12-11-031":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0031 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Germany Declares War on the United States</h1><div class=\"meta\">1941-12-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The European and Pacific wars become even more closely connected.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1941-12-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-01-124":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0124 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Declaration by United Nations</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-01 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Twenty-six states reaffirm their common struggle against the Axis powers and pledge not to make a separate peace.</p><p>The document was signed in Washington as the Allies coordinated their war effort. At that time, the term &#x27;United Nations&#x27; referred to the coalition fighting the Axis rather than the post-war international organisation.</p><p>In the declaration, the signatories agreed to devote their resources to the war and not to negotiate a separate peace with the enemy. For a coalition of states with very different political systems, this was an essential minimum of shared commitments.</p><p>The declaration became an important step toward the creation of the United Nations in 1945. It also marked a transition from individual alliance treaties to broader global coordination.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-02-125":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0125 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Japanese Forces Capture Manila</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-02 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Japanese forces enter the Philippine capital; American and Filipino troops withdraw to Bataan.</p><p>The capture of Manila was part of the Japanese offensive in the Philippines after the attacks of December 1941. The defenders abandoned the city and tried to concentrate resistance on the Bataan Peninsula and Corregidor Island.</p><p>The fall of the capital did not end the campaign. US and Philippine forces continued to defend themselves amid shortages of supplies, food and medicine.</p><p>The fighting in the Philippines demonstrated how rapidly Japan was expanding its control across Southeast Asia in early 1942.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-07-126":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0126 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Failure of the German Offensive on Moscow</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-07 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Operation Typhoon fails to achieve its objective as the Soviet counteroffensive pushes Wehrmacht forces back from the capital.</p><p>The Battle of Moscow developed in the autumn of 1941, when German armies attempted to seize the Soviet capital before winter. Exhaustion, logistical problems and Soviet resistance halted the advance.</p><p>In December, the Red Army went over to the counteroffensive, and by early January it was clear that the German plan for a rapid capture of Moscow had failed. The front nevertheless remained extremely tense.</p><p>This was an important strategic check on the German offensive, but it did not end the fighting on the Eastern Front. New campaigns would follow in 1942.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-14-ind-041":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-041 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Sikorsky XR-4 — Prototype of the First Production Helicopter</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">In April, the XR-4 reached about 1,520 m; during a delivery flight it covered about 1,225 km.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 14 January 1942, in United States, the milestone “First Flight of the Sikorsky XR-4 — Prototype of the First Production Helicopter” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: In April, the XR-4 reached about 1,520 m; during a delivery flight it covered about 1,225 km. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/vought-sikorsky-xr-4c/nasm_A19600307000\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-16-new-029":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-029 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Deportations from the Łódź Ghetto to Chełmno Begin</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-16 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Nazis begin systematic deportations of Jews to be murdered.</p><p>The Nazis begin systematic deportations of Jews to be murdered.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/1942-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-20-032":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0032 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Wannsee Conference</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-20 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Nazi officials coordinate the implementation of the policy to systematically murder Europe&#x27;s Jews.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline/holocaust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-23-127":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0127 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Japanese Landing at Rabaul</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Japanese forces begin the capture of Rabaul on New Britain.</p><p>Rabaul&#x27;s port was well placed to control sea and air communications in the south-west Pacific. It was defended by an Australian garrison.</p><p>Japan&#x27;s superiority in numbers and air support allowed its forces to overcome organised defence quickly. Some defenders were killed or captured, while others tried to retreat into the island&#x27;s interior.</p><p>Rabaul later became one of Japan&#x27;s principal bases in the region and an important target of the Allied strategy of isolating enemy strongholds.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-26-128":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0128 · Western Europe</div><h1>First US Troops Arrive in Northern Ireland</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-26 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A US Army contingent lands in Northern Ireland after the United States enters the war.</p><p>After the attack on Pearl Harbor, the United States became a full participant in the global war. Sending troops to the British Isles was part of preparations for future operations in Europe.</p><p>The arrival had both symbolic and practical importance: the Allies organised accommodation, training, supply and joint command arrangements.</p><p>The large-scale landing in France would not take place until 1944, but the infrastructure needed for it was being created long beforehand.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-26\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-01-31-129":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0129 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Allied Forces Leave the Malay Peninsula</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-01-31 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">British and Allied units withdraw to Singapore after the Japanese advance through Malaya.</p><p>The Japanese campaign down the Malay Peninsula advanced rapidly southward from December 1941. Allied forces steadily lost the initiative under pressure from mobile Japanese formations.</p><p>By the end of January, the defenders abandoned their mainland positions and concentrated on Singapore Island; the causeway linking it to the mainland was partly destroyed.</p><p>The withdrawal set the stage for the decisive battle for Singapore. Its fall in February became one of Britain&#x27;s most serious defeats of the war.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-01-31\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-02-15-033":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0033 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Fall of Singapore</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-02-15 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The British garrison at Singapore surrenders to Japanese forces.</p><p>The surrender of the large British garrison became one of Britain&#x27;s gravest colonial military defeats. Japan gained a strategic port and strengthened its control of the Malay Peninsula.</p><p>The Japanese advance through Malaya shattered British assumptions about the fortress&#x27;s impregnability. Allied forces retreated toward Singapore under constant pressure from air and ground attacks.</p><p>After heavy fighting, the command decided to surrender. Tens of thousands of servicemen became prisoners of war, and many were subjected to extremely brutal treatment.</p><p>The defeat dealt a major blow to British imperial prestige and changed the balance of power in Southeast Asia.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-02-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-03-01-130":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0130 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Second Battle of the Java Sea</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-03-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Japanese forces destroy Allied ships attempting to escape the Java area.</p><p>After Allied naval defeats in the waters around Java, surviving ships attempted to break through to safer bases. The Japanese fleet controlled the principal withdrawal routes.</p><p>The British ships HMS Exeter and HMS Encounter and the American USS Pope were sunk. The engagement formed part of the rapid collapse of the naval defence of the Dutch East Indies.</p><p>The loss of these warships further reduced the Allies&#x27; ability to impede the Japanese occupation of the islands.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-03-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-03-27-new-030":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-030 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Deportations of Jews from France to Auschwitz Begin</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-03-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Deportations from occupied France were directed mainly to Auschwitz.</p><p>Deportations from occupied France were directed mainly to Auschwitz.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/1942-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-03-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-04-09-034":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0034 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Surrender on Bataan</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-04-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American and Filipino forces surrender to Japan; the Bataan Death March follows.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-04-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-04-18-035":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0035 · Air War</div><h1>Doolittle Raid</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-04-18 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American bombers carry out the first US air strike on the Japanese home islands.</p><p>American B-25 bombers took off from an aircraft carrier for a symbolic strike on Japanese cities. The direct military effect was limited, but the raid had major psychological significance.</p><p>B-25 aircraft launched from the carrier Hornet under conditions for which such bombers were not normally designed. The principal aim was psychological after the attack on Pearl Harbor.</p><p>Material damage was limited, and the crews faced a difficult journey after the raid to reach landing grounds in China or make forced landings.</p><p>The raid raised morale in the United States and forced the Japanese command to reconsider the security of the home islands.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-04-18\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-05-04-036":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0036 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of the Coral Sea</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-05-04 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">US and Japanese carrier forces fight a battle in which the opposing surface fleets never directly exchange gunfire.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-05-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-05-25-ind-042":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-042 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Commercial Fluid Catalytic Cracking Reactor Starts Up</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-05-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Feed throughput: about 2.23 million litres per day.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 25 May 1942, in United States, the milestone “First Commercial Fluid Catalytic Cracking Reactor Starts Up” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Feed throughput: about 2.23 million litres per day. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This oil refining / chemical engineering equipment milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.acs.org/education/whatischemistry/landmarks/fluidbedreactor.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-05-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-05-30-new008":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0208 · Air War</div><h1>First British Thousand-Bomber Raid on Cologne</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-05-30 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">In 1942, RAF Bomber Command intensified strategic air attacks on Germany&#x27;s industrial cities.</p><p>In 1942, RAF Bomber Command intensified strategic air attacks on Germany&#x27;s industrial cities.</p><p>On the night of 30–31 May, a mass raid on Cologne was carried out with more than one thousand British bombers participating.</p><p>The operation demonstrated the scale of Britain&#x27;s air power, but mass bombing of cities also caused civilian deaths.</p><p>The raid became a landmark episode in the prolonged Allied air campaign against Germany; the consequences and legitimacy of particular bombing methods remain subjects of historical study.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-05-30\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-06-04-037":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0037 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of Midway</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-06-04 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The US Navy wins a major victory over Japanese carrier forces.</p><p>In June 1942, the Japanese command planned to seize Midway Atoll and provoke a decisive engagement with American aircraft carriers. US intelligence obtained important information about the Japanese plan.</p><p>On 4 June, American carrier aircraft launched a series of strikes against the Japanese carrier force. During the campaign, Japan lost four aircraft carriers.</p><p>The United States also suffered losses, including the carrier Yorktown. Victory resulted from intelligence, operational decisions and the actions of aircrews under difficult conditions.</p><p>Midway is often described as one of the principal turning points of the Pacific War: Japan lost experienced air groups and a substantial portion of its offensive carrier strength.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-06-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-06-28-new-043":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-043 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Operation Blue Begins</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-06-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Wehrmacht launches its summer offensive in the southern sector of the Eastern Front.</p><p>The Wehrmacht launches its summer offensive in the southern sector of the Eastern Front.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-06-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-07-15-new-031":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-031 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Systematic Deportations of Jews from the Netherlands Begin</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-07-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Transports departed for the killing centres.</p><p>Transports departed for the killing centres.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/1942-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-07-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-07-17-038":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0038 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Battle of Stalingrad Begins</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-07-17 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Prolonged fighting begins in the Stalingrad area.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-07-17\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-07-18-ind-043":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-043 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Messerschmitt Me 262 Makes Its First Flight on Jet Power Alone</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-07-18 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A key milestone for the first production jet fighter.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 18 July 1942, in Germany, the milestone “Messerschmitt Me 262 Makes Its First Flight on Jet Power Alone” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A key milestone for the first production jet fighter. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / jet engines milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/museums/nnam/explore/collections/aircraft/m/me-262.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-07-18\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-07-22-new-033":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-033 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Grossaktion in the Warsaw Ghetto Begins</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-07-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Mass deportations to Treblinka begin.</p><p>Mass deportations to Treblinka begin.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-07-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-08-07-039":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0039 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Landings on Guadalcanal</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-08-07 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The United States begins an offensive operation in the Solomon Islands.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-08-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-08-13-ind-044":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-044 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Manhattan Engineer District Formally Established</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-08-13 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Organisational start of the Oak Ridge and Hanford industrial sites.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 13 August 1942, in United States, the milestone “Manhattan Engineer District Formally Established” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Organisational start of the Oak Ridge and Hanford industrial sites. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry / megaprojects milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/mapr/faqs.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-08-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-08-19-105":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0105 · Western Europe</div><h1>Dieppe Raid</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-08-19 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Allies carry out an unsuccessful amphibious raid on the French coast.</p><p>The operation was intended to test the feasibility of landing on a fortified coast and to gather practical experience. Canadian units bore the main burden of the fighting.</p><p>Strong German defences and problems with intelligence, landing arrangements and support led to heavy losses. Many troops were killed or taken prisoner.</p><p>The experience gained at Dieppe became important material for planning later amphibious assaults, although historians continue to debate the raid&#x27;s strategic justification.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-08-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-08-23-040":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0040 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Massive Air Raid on Stalingrad</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-08-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Heavy bombing strikes the city as fighting intensifies in its approaches.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-08-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-10-03-ind-046":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-046 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Successful Full-Scale Test of the V-2 Rocket</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-10-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Length about 14 m; mass about 13.2 t; speed over 5,600 km/h.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 3 October 1942, in Germany, the milestone “First Successful Full-Scale Test of the V-2 Rocket” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Length about 14 m; mass about 13.2 t; speed over 5,600 km/h. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This rocket engineering milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>This industrial history is inseparable from the Nazi war economy. The related article must also address coercion and forced labour where documented; the technical achievement is not presented as a value-neutral success.</p><p>Editorial status: Month and core fact verified; no unsupported day assigned. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/people/wernher-von-braun/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-10-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-10-23-041":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0041 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Second Battle of El Alamein</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-10-23 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Allied forces go on the offensive in a battle that becomes a turning point in North Africa.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-10-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-11-08-042":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0042 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Operation Torch</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-11-08 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Anglo-American forces land in French North Africa.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-11-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-11-11-106":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0106 · Western Europe</div><h1>Germany Occupies the Unoccupied Zone of France</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-11-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the Allied landings in North Africa, German troops extend the occupation into southern France.</p><p>Until then, the Vichy regime had formally administered part of France without direct German occupation. The changing situation in the Mediterranean prompted Berlin to act quickly.</p><p>German and Italian units occupied the previously unoccupied zone. The French fleet at Toulon was later scuttled by its crews to prevent its capture.</p><p>These events finally demonstrated the limits of Vichy sovereignty and intensified the struggle of the French Resistance.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1942)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-11-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-11-12-ind-047":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-047 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Liberty Ship SS Robert E. Peary Sets a Construction-Speed Record</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-11-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">From keel laying to launch: 4 days, 15 hours, 29 minutes.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 12 November 1942, in United States, the milestone “Liberty Ship SS Robert E. Peary Sets a Construction-Speed Record” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: From keel laying to launch: 4 days, 15 hours, 29 minutes. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This shipbuilding / mass production milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and record verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://home.nps.gov/articles/s-s-scotts-bluff-forgotten-contributor.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-11-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-11-19-043":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0043 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Operation Uranus</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-11-19 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces launch a counteroffensive at Stalingrad, threatening Axis troops with encirclement.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-11-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-11-20-ind-048":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-048 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Alaska Highway Officially Opened</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-11-20 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Original route: more than 2,400 km.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 20 November 1942, in Canada / United States, the milestone “Alaska Highway Officially Opened” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Original route: more than 2,400 km. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This road infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.loc.gov/item/today-in-history/november-20/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-11-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1942-11-23-new-045":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-045 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Encirclement Ring Closes at Stalingrad</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-11-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet fronts link up near Kalach-on-Don.</p><p>Soviet fronts link up near Kalach-on-Don.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-11-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","world-1942-12-02-02":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №1002 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Controlled Self-Sustaining Nuclear Chain Reaction</h1><div class=\"meta\">1942-12-02 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Under Enrico Fermi&#x27;s leadership, Chicago Pile-1 achieved the first controlled self-sustaining nuclear chain reaction, a major milestone in experimental nuclear physics.</p><p>Under Enrico Fermi&#x27;s leadership, Chicago Pile-1 achieved a controlled self-sustaining nuclear chain reaction in Chicago. It was a major milestone in experimental nuclear physics.</p><p>The pile contained graphite and uranium, with the reaction controlled by neutron-absorbing rods. The event opened the practical era of nuclear reactors while also being directly connected to wartime research.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/7-moments-december-changed-nuclear-energy-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1942-12-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-01-09-ind-051":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-051 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Lockheed Constellation</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-01-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A future symbol of post-war long-range piston-powered aviation.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 9 January 1943, in United States, the milestone “First Flight of the Lockheed Constellation” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A future symbol of post-war long-range piston-powered aviation. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This civil aviation / aircraft manufacturing milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/lockheed-1049f-55-96-constellation/nasm_A19880371000\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-01-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-02-02-044":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0044 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Battle of Stalingrad Ends</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-02-02 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The remaining forces of the encircled German Sixth Army surrender.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-02-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-02-09-045":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0045 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of Guadalcanal Ends</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-02-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The United States secures control of the island after Japanese forces withdraw.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-02-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-02-18-ind-052":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-052 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Construction Begins on the First Y-12 Production Building at Oak Ridge</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-02-18 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Start of a major electromagnetic uranium-isotope separation complex.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 18 February 1943, in United States, the milestone “Construction Begins on the First Y-12 Production Building at Oak Ridge” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Start of a major electromagnetic uranium-isotope separation complex. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.y12.doe.gov/about/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-02-18\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-03-05-ind-053":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-053 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Gloster Meteor</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-03-05 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The aircraft that would become Britain&#x27;s first production jet aeroplane.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 5 March 1943, in United Kingdom, the milestone “First Flight of the Gloster Meteor” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The aircraft that would become Britain&#x27;s first production jet aeroplane. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / jet technology milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.baesystems.com/en/heritage/gloster-meteor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-03-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-03-22-ind-054":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-054 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Major Construction Begins at the Hanford Industrial Complex</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-03-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Start of the first complex for large-scale plutonium production.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 22 March 1943, in United States, the milestone “Major Construction Begins at the Hanford Industrial Complex” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Start of the first complex for large-scale plutonium production. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry / infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/mapr/learn/photosmultimedia/hanford-t-plant-virtual-tour.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-03-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","world-1943-04-09-03":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №1003 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Contract Signed to Build ENIAC</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-04-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Moore School of Electrical Engineering at the University of Pennsylvania and the US Army sign a contract to develop the ENIAC electronic computer. One of its intended tasks is artillery ballistic calculation.</p><p>The Moore School of Electrical Engineering at the University of Pennsylvania and the US Army signed a contract to develop the ENIAC electronic computing machine. One of its intended tasks was artillery ballistic calculation.</p><p>This is the date of the contract, not the date the computer entered service or was publicly unveiled. The project was completed after the war and influenced the later history of digital computing.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computerhistory.org/tdih/april/9/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-04-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-04-19-046":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0046 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Warsaw Ghetto Uprising</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-04-19 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Jewish underground fighters begin armed resistance to the Nazi liquidation of the ghetto.</p><p>Fighters from the Jewish Combat Organization and other groups offered armed resistance to the German liquidation of the ghetto. The Nazis crushed the uprising, destroyed the district and deported or murdered many of its inhabitants.</p><p>After mass deportations to the killing centres, the remaining Jewish community of Warsaw faced the threat of final destruction. Underground organisations prepared weapons and defensive positions.</p><p>German forces used artillery, fire and systematic destruction of buildings to suppress the resistance. The fighters resisted despite an overwhelming imbalance of forces.</p><p>The uprising became a defining symbol of resistance during the Holocaust; most of its fighters and ghetto inhabitants were killed or deported.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline/holocaust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-04-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-05-13-047":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0047 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Axis Surrender in Tunisia</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-05-13 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">German and Italian forces end organised resistance in North Africa.</p><p>After their routes of retreat were blocked in Tunisia, German and Italian forces surrendered to the Allies. The victory ended the North African campaign and prepared the way for the invasion of Italy.</p><p>After the Second Battle of El Alamein and the Allied landings in Morocco and Algeria, Axis forces were pressed from both directions. In Tunisia, the defensive force gradually lost room to manoeuvre.</p><p>The Allies captured large numbers of enemy troops and secured control of the southern part of the Mediterranean theatre.</p><p>The end of the campaign opened the way to the invasion of Sicily and the Italian mainland.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-05-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-06-29-ind-056":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-056 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the First Production B-29 Built at Wichita</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-06-29 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A milestone for one of the most complex mass-produced aircraft of the war.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 29 June 1943, in United States, the milestone “First Flight of the First Production B-29 Built at Wichita” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A milestone for one of the most complex mass-produced aircraft of the war. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / mass production milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://home.nps.gov/articles/000/h-our-history-aviation-production-in-wichita-ks-wwii-heritage-city.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-06-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-07-05-048":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0048 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Battle of Kursk</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-07-05 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany begins an offensive against the Kursk salient; the subsequent Soviet counteroffensive changes the situation on the front.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-07-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-07-10-049":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0049 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Allied Landings in Sicily</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-07-10 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Allies begin a major amphibious invasion of the island.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-07-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-07-25-109":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0109 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Fall of Mussolini&#x27;s Regime</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-07-25 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Italian dictator is removed from office after a decision by the Grand Council of Fascism.</p><p>Military defeats, bombing and the Allied invasion of Sicily undermined support for the Fascist leadership. King Victor Emmanuel III asked Pietro Badoglio to form a new government.</p><p>Mussolini was arrested, but Italy did not immediately leave the war. The new leadership began secret negotiations with the Allies.</p><p>The subsequent armistice brought German occupation to much of Italy and a prolonged struggle on Italian territory.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-07-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-08-14-ind-057":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-057 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Big Inch Oil Pipeline Completed</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-08-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The trunk line stretched for more than 2,000 km.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 14 August 1943, in United States, the milestone “Big Inch Oil Pipeline Completed” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The trunk line stretched for more than 2,000 km. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This oil infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/big-inch-and-little-big-inch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-08-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-03-050":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0050 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Allied Invasion of Mainland Italy</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Anglo-American forces land on the Italian mainland.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-08-051":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0051 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Italian Armistice Announced</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-08 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Italian government announces an armistice with the Allies; Germany occupies much of the country.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-09-136":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0136 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Allied Landings at Salerno</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-09 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Anglo-American forces land in the Salerno area of mainland Italy.</p><p>After the Sicily campaign, the Allies sought a foothold on the Italian mainland. Italy&#x27;s armistice was announced publicly on 8 September, but German forces continued to resist.</p><p>On 9 September, Allied troops landed near Salerno in Operation Avalanche. German units counterattacked the beachhead, attempting to drive the landing force into the sea.</p><p>With support from naval gunfire and aircraft, the landed forces held their positions. By mid-September they linked up with other Allied units.</p><p>The operation opened a prolonged Italian campaign in which difficult terrain and prepared defensive lines slowed the Allied advance.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-10-137":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0137 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>German Forces Occupy Rome</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-10 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the Italian armistice, German troops take control of Rome.</p><p>On 8 September 1943, the Italian armistice with the Allies was announced. Germany had prepared plans to disarm Italian forces and seize key cities.</p><p>On 10 September, German troops took control of Rome despite resistance by some Italian soldiers and civilians. The king and government left the capital.</p><p>The occupation placed the city&#x27;s population under Nazi rule. Persecution of resistance members and Jews intensified.</p><p>The Allies entered Rome only in June 1944, after prolonged fighting in central Italy.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-16-138":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0138 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Allied Forces Link Up near Salerno</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-16 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Allied troops consolidate the Salerno beachhead.</p><p>The 9 September landings did not guarantee success: German formations launched strong counterattacks around the Allied perimeter.</p><p>By mid-September, British and American troops had held their positions. On 16 September, their units linked up in the Salerno area.</p><p>German forces began withdrawing to prepared defensive lines while retaining the ability to delay the Allied advance.</p><p>After Salerno, the campaign became a succession of assaults against defensive positions in difficult mountainous terrain.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-25-139":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0139 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Red Army Retakes Smolensk</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet troops recapture Smolensk after offensive operations.</p><p>After the German defeat at Kursk, the Soviet command intensified offensives on several sectors. Smolensk remained an important communications hub.</p><p>On 25 September, Soviet forces entered the city. German troops withdrew while attempting to preserve an organised defence farther west.</p><p>Smolensk had been under occupation since 1941. Liberated areas revealed the severe consequences of occupation policies and wartime destruction.</p><p>The advance toward Smolensk altered the situation on the central sector of the Eastern Front ahead of the major operations of 1944.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-28-140":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0140 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Four Days of Naples Uprising</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Residents of Naples rise against German occupation forces.</p><p>After Italy&#x27;s armistice, Germany occupied important parts of the country. Tensions in Naples grew under coercive measures by the occupation authorities.</p><p>At the end of September, residents launched an armed uprising known as the Four Days of Naples. Civilians from different social groups joined the resistance.</p><p>German forces left Naples before Allied troops entered. The city suffered serious destruction and the severe consequences of occupation.</p><p>The uprising became a symbol of Italian anti-fascist resistance and of the recovery of political agency after the fall of Mussolini&#x27;s regime.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-09-29-ind-058":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-058 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Polymer Corporation Begins Industrial Production of Synthetic Rubber</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-09-29 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Later production capacity exceeded about 36,000 t per year.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 29 September 1943, in Canada, the milestone “Polymer Corporation Begins Industrial Production of Synthetic Rubber” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Later production capacity exceeded about 36,000 t per year. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This chemical industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_nhs_eng.aspx?id=10954\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-09-29\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-10-01-new-034":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-034 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Rescue of the Danish Jews</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-10-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Danish resistance and ordinary citizens begin a mass effort to ferry Jews to Sweden.</p><p>The Danish resistance and ordinary citizens begin a mass effort to ferry Jews to Sweden.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-10-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-10-13-110":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0110 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Italy Declares War on Germany</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-10-13 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Italian government enters the war against its former ally on the side of the anti-Hitler coalition.</p><p>After the armistice with the Allies and the German occupation of much of the country, Italy experienced a political and military split. In the north, a fascist regime dependent on Berlin remained in place.</p><p>Some Italian troops and partisans cooperated with the Allies. At the same time, the campaign in Italy remained bitter and destructive for civilians.</p><p>The war in Italy continued until spring 1945, combining conventional front-line operations, resistance to occupation and civil conflict.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1943)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-10-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-10-19-worldnew003":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew003 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Discovery of the Antibiotic Streptomycin</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-10-19 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Researchers at Rutgers identified a substance that became important in the treatment of tuberculosis.</p><p>On 19 October 1943, Albert Schatz, a graduate student in Selman Waksman&#x27;s laboratory at Rutgers University, isolated streptomycin. The research grew from a systematic search for substances produced by soil microorganisms.</p><p>Unlike penicillin, streptomycin proved active against the bacterium that causes tuberculosis. This was especially significant at a time when tuberculosis remained widespread and often fatal.</p><p>The path from laboratory discovery to widespread treatment required extensive testing of effectiveness, toxicity and methods of production. Other members of the research group, including Elizabeth Bugie, also contributed; the later allocation of credit became controversial.</p><p>Streptomycin became one of the symbols of the early antibiotic era. The history of its discovery is a reminder that scientific breakthroughs are rarely the work of only one person.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://sebsnjaesnews.rutgers.edu/2013/09/famous-author-visits-rutgers-marks-70th-anniversary-of-streptomycin-discovery/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-10-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-11-03-ind-059":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-059 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Willow Run Completes Its 1,000th B-24 Bomber</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-11-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The 1,000th completed B-24; a precursor to the peak assembly-line rate reached in 1944.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 3 November 1943, in United States, the milestone “Willow Run Completes Its 1,000th B-24 Bomber” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The 1,000th completed B-24; a precursor to the peak assembly-line rate reached in 1944. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / mass production milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/sets/detail/willow-run-bomber-plant\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-11-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-11-04-ind-060":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-060 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>X-10 Graphite Reactor Begins Operation at Oak Ridge</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-11-04 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The first reactor to produce plutonium continuously on a pilot-industrial scale.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 4 November 1943, in United States, the milestone “X-10 Graphite Reactor Begins Operation at Oak Ridge” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The first reactor to produce plutonium continuously on a pilot-industrial scale. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.energy.gov/management/x-10-graphite-reactor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-11-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-11-06-052":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0052 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Liberation of Kyiv</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-11-06 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces recapture Kyiv from the Nazi occupiers.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-11-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1943-11-28-053":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0053 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Tehran Conference</h1><div class=\"meta\">1943-11-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The leaders of the Soviet Union, the United States and Britain discuss a common strategy for the war.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1943-11-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-01-22-054":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0054 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Anzio Landings</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-01-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American and British forces land behind German defensive positions in Italy.</p><p>The landing was intended to bypass German defences on the road to Rome, but Allied troops remained confined to the beachhead for months. The fighting at Anzio demonstrated the risks of an amphibious operation without rapid exploitation of initial success.</p><p>Allied planners hoped to bypass the Gustav Line and accelerate the advance toward Rome. The landing initially encountered limited resistance, but rapidly reinforced German forces contained the beachhead.</p><p>Instead of a quick breakout, prolonged fighting and artillery duels followed. Only in May were the Allies able to break through the surrounding positions.</p><p>The operation demonstrated the difficulty of turning an amphibious landing into a rapid operational breakthrough and became an important phase of the Italian campaign.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-01-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-01-27-055":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0055 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Siege of Leningrad Fully Lifted</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-01-27 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces push German troops away from Leningrad and bring the siege of the city to an end.</p><p>On 27 January 1944, Soviet forces completed the lifting of the siege of Leningrad after a prolonged blockade. This date should not be confused with the partial breaking of the blockade ring in January 1943.</p><p>The January offensive by the Leningrad and Volkhov fronts forced German troops to withdraw from positions around the city. Communications, partly restored in 1943, became much safer.</p><p>The siege caused extraordinary civilian losses, above all through starvation, cold and shelling. Its end had immense humanitarian and symbolic significance.</p><p>The defence of Leningrad and the suffering of its inhabitants became one of the central themes in the history and memory of the war on the Eastern Front.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-01-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-02-05-ind-061":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-061 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Colossus Mk I Begins Operation at Bletchley Park</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-02-05 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of the first programmable electronic digital computers.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 5 February 1944, in United Kingdom, the milestone “Colossus Mk I Begins Operation at Bletchley Park” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: One of the first programmable electronic digital computers. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This electronics / computing milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.tnmoc.org/news-releases/2019/5/29/celebration-of-the-75th-anniversary-of-colossus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-02-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-03-02-ind-062":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-062 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Little Big Inch Products Pipeline Completed</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-03-02 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Together with Big Inch it formed a strategic fuel-delivery system to the eastern United States.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 2 March 1944, in United States, the milestone “Little Big Inch Products Pipeline Completed” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Together with Big Inch it formed a strategic fuel-delivery system to the eastern United States. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This oil infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/big-inch-and-little-big-inch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-03-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-03-19-new009":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0209 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Germany Occupies Hungary</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-03-19 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">By early 1944, Germany&#x27;s leadership feared that Hungary might try to leave the Axis alliance.</p><p>By early 1944, Germany&#x27;s leadership feared that Hungary might try to leave the Axis alliance.</p><p>On 19 March, German troops occupied Hungary. The occupation brought a change of government and tighter German control over the country.</p><p>The consequences were catastrophic for Hungarian Jews: mass deportations to Nazi killing centres began after the occupation.</p><p>German intervention increased Hungary&#x27;s dependence on the Nazi regime at a time when Germany&#x27;s military situation was steadily worsening.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-03-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-04-08-056":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0056 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Crimean Offensive</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-04-08 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces begin driving German and Romanian troops out of Crimea.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-04-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-04-10-new-017":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-017 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Nazi Forces Driven from Odesa</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-04-10 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces retake the city during the Odesa Offensive.</p><p>Soviet forces retake the city during the Odesa Offensive.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/odessa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-04-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-04-14-ind-063":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-063 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Hydro-Québec Created</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-04-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A key step in centralisation and the later expansion of Quebec&#x27;s hydroelectric system.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 14 April 1944, in Canada, the milestone “Hydro-Québec Created” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A key step in centralisation and the later expansion of Quebec&#x27;s hydroelectric system. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This energy / national infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.hydroquebec.com/history-electricity-in-quebec/timeline/toward-nationalization.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-04-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-06-04-057":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0057 · Mediterranean and Africa</div><h1>Liberation of Rome</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-06-04 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Allied forces enter the Italian capital.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-06-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-06-06-058":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0058 · Western Europe</div><h1>Normandy Landings — D-Day</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-06-06 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Allies open a major front in Western Europe.</p><p>The Allies prepared the invasion of France for a long time, concentrating troops, landing craft and supplies in Britain. Success depended on control of the air and sea and on deceiving German intelligence.</p><p>On 6 June 1944, airborne and seaborne forces landed in Normandy. American, British, Canadian and other Allied troops attacked several beach sectors.</p><p>Resistance varied by sector; fighting was especially severe on Omaha Beach. Over the following weeks the Allies expanded the bridgehead while overcoming German defences in the Normandy bocage.</p><p>The operation opened a major front in Western Europe and became a crucial step toward the liberation of France and the subsequent advance into Germany.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-06-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-06-19-060":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0060 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of the Philippine Sea</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-06-19 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Japanese carrier aviation suffers a major defeat.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-06-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-06-22-059":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0059 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Operation Bagration</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-06-22 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A massive Soviet offensive begins in Belarus.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-06-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-07-01-new-118":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-118 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Bretton Woods Conference Opens</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-07-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Delegates work on a new international financial system.</p><p>Delegates work on a new international financial system.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/043.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-07-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-07-20-061":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0061 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Failed Plot to Assassinate Hitler</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-07-20 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A conspiracy by German military and civilian opponents of the regime fails.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-07-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-07-25-new010":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0210 · Western Europe</div><h1>Allied Breakout from Normandy — Operation Cobra</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-07-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the 6 June 1944 landings, Allied troops fought for weeks in Normandy and struggled to advance rapidly into France.</p><p>After the 6 June 1944 landings, Allied troops fought for weeks in Normandy and struggled to advance rapidly into France.</p><p>On 25 July, American forces launched Operation Cobra, an offensive that broke through German defences and opened the way to mobile warfare.</p><p>The offensive relied on concentrated ground forces and air support. Its success allowed the Allies to advance more rapidly eastward across France.</p><p>The breakout from the Normandy bridgehead became a major stage in the liberation of France and the subsequent advance toward Germany&#x27;s borders.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-07-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-07-25-ind-065":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-065 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Confirmed Air Combat by the Jet-Powered Me 262</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-07-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Jet aviation moves from testing into combat use.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 25 July 1944, in Germany / Western Europe, the milestone “First Confirmed Air Combat by the Jet-Powered Me 262” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Jet aviation moves from testing into combat use. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / jet technology milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/research-enquiries/history-of-aviation-timeline/world-aviation/1944-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-07-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-08-01-062":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0062 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Warsaw Uprising</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-08-01 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Polish Home Army begins an uprising against German occupation in Warsaw.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-08-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-08-07-worldnew004":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew004 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Harvard Mark I Presented to Harvard University</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-08-07 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">IBM formally presents one of the early automatic digital calculators to the university.</p><p>On 7 August 1944, IBM president Thomas Watson Sr formally presented Harvard University with the Automatic Sequence Controlled Calculator, known as the Harvard Mark I. The machine was the result of a long development process associated with Howard Aiken&#x27;s ideas.</p><p>The large electromechanical calculator used relay and mechanical components and could execute sequences of operations automatically. Its physical size illustrates how different early computers were from modern electronic devices.</p><p>Although vacuum-tube electronics soon surpassed electromechanical systems in speed, the Mark I played an important role in the development of automated computation and in training a new generation of specialists.</p><p>The date 7 August marks the machine&#x27;s formal presentation and transfer, not the first conception or the start of funding. Both stages are important to understanding the long development of computing technology.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computerhistory.org/tdih/august/7/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-08-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-08-15-063":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0063 · Western Europe</div><h1>Allied Landings in Southern France</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-08-15 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Allied troops land on France&#x27;s Mediterranean coast.</p><p>Operation Dragoon complemented the Normandy invasion with an offensive in southern France. The Allies gained important Mediterranean ports and accelerated the expulsion of German forces from the country.</p><p>The operation was intended to open another axis of advance and secure major ports for supplying Allied armies. The landing was supported by French forces and the local Resistance.</p><p>German forces withdrew up the Rhône valley in an effort to avoid encirclement. The Allies quickly seized important ports and advanced north.</p><p>The new front complemented the advance from Normandy and helped accelerate the liberation of France.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-08-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-08-21-new-119":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-119 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Dumbarton Oaks Conference Begins</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-08-21 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Representatives of major powers develop plans for the future United Nations.</p><p>Representatives of major powers develop plans for the future United Nations.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.un.org/en/about-us/history-of-the-un/preparatory-years\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-08-21\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-08-25-064":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0064 · Western Europe</div><h1>Liberation of Paris</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-08-25 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">French and Allied forces restore control over Paris.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-08-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-09-01-ind-067":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-067 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Volvo Unveils the PV444</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-09-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The model laid the foundation for Volvo&#x27;s post-war mass car production.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 September 1944, in Sweden, the milestone “Volvo Unveils the PV444” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The model laid the foundation for Volvo&#x27;s post-war mass car production. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.volvocars.com/intl/media/press-releases/69BCECD5F2AFA1C3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-09-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-09-09-ind-068":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-068 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Willow Run Produces Its 6,000th B-24</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-09-09 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">At peak output: approximately one B-24 every 63 minutes.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 9 September 1944, in United States, the milestone “Willow Run Produces Its 6,000th B-24” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: At peak output: approximately one B-24 every 63 minutes. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / mass production milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/artifact/444348\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-09-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-09-17-114":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0114 · Western Europe</div><h1>Operation Market Garden</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-09-17 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Allies attempt to seize bridges in the Netherlands with airborne troops and a ground advance.</p><p>The plan aimed to cross several river barriers rapidly and reach Germany around established defensive positions. Airborne divisions landed near key bridges.</p><p>The ground advance was delayed, while British paratroopers at Arnhem faced stronger opposition than expected. The most distant bridge could not be held.</p><p>The operation produced local gains but failed to achieve its principal strategic objective. The war on the Western Front continued into the following winter.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1944)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-09-17\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-09-22-ind-069":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-069 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First PLUTO Pipeline to Cherbourg Begins Delivering Fuel</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-09-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Initial throughput: approximately 255,000 litres per day.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 22 September 1944, in United Kingdom / France, the milestone “First PLUTO Pipeline to Cherbourg Begins Delivering Fuel” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Initial throughput: approximately 255,000 litres per day. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This fuel infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.usni.org/magazines/proceedings/1954/june/operation-pluto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-09-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-09-26-ind-070":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-070 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Hanford B Reactor Reaches Criticality</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-09-26 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The first large-scale reactor for industrial plutonium production.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 26 September 1944, in United States, the milestone “Hanford B Reactor Reaches Criticality” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The first large-scale reactor for industrial plutonium production. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.energy.gov/documents/b-reactor-fact-sheet-79-finalpdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-09-26\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-10-02-141":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0141 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Warsaw Uprising Ends</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-10-02 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After two months of fighting, Polish insurgents end organised resistance.</p><p>The Warsaw Uprising began on 1 August 1944 on the initiative of the Home Army. Its participants sought to liberate the capital from German occupation before Soviet forces established control.</p><p>German forces brutally suppressed the resistance and committed mass killings of civilians. The insurgents ran short of ammunition, while outside aid was insufficient.</p><p>On 2 October, an agreement was concluded for the surrender of the insurgent forces. Much of the population was deported and the city was systematically destroyed.</p><p>The defeat became one of the most tragic chapters in Poland&#x27;s wartime history; its political consequences remain the subject of historical debate.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1944)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-10-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-10-06-143":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0143 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Fighting at the Dukla Pass</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-10-06 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet and Czechoslovak troops enter Slovakia.</p><p>The Carpathian sector gained importance after the Slovak National Uprising. An offensive through mountain passes required major effort and difficult logistics.</p><p>In early October, Soviet and Czechoslovak units reached Slovak territory while continuing to fight in the Dukla sector.</p><p>Mountain terrain, weather and German defences slowed the advance, and both sides suffered heavy losses.</p><p>The Dukla operation later became an important element of Czechoslovak historical memory about liberation from Nazi occupation.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1944)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-10-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-10-07-142":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0142 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Sonderkommando Uprising at Auschwitz</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-10-07 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Prisoners of the Sonderkommando stage an uprising at Auschwitz-Birkenau.</p><p>The Nazi system of mass murder forced Sonderkommando prisoners to work near the gas chambers and crematoria. Members of these units lived under constant threat of being killed themselves.</p><p>On 7 October 1944, some prisoners revolted and damaged one of the crematoria. Participants attempted to resist the guards and escape from the camp.</p><p>The uprising was suppressed and many of its participants were killed. The episode demonstrates that organised resistance existed even under extraordinarily brutal conditions.</p><p>The event has particular importance in Holocaust history and should be understood through survivor testimony, camp archives and historical research.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1944)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-10-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-10-20-065":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0065 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Landings on Leyte</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-10-20 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American forces begin the return to the Philippines with landings on Leyte.</p><p>American forces landed on the Philippine island of Leyte, beginning a major campaign to retake the Philippines. The offensive threatened Japanese sea communications and access to the resources of Southeast Asia.</p><p>After a prolonged Pacific campaign, the Allies returned to the Philippines, which Japan had occupied early in the war. The landings were supported by massive naval and air forces.</p><p>The Japanese fleet attempted to disrupt the operation, leading to a series of battles in Leyte Gulf. On land, the campaign continued through difficult terrain with heavy losses.</p><p>The return to the Philippines weakened Japan&#x27;s communications with resource-producing areas of Southeast Asia.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-10-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-10-23-066":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0066 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of Leyte Gulf</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-10-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of the largest naval battles in history.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-10-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1944-12-16-067":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0067 · Western Europe</div><h1>Battle of the Bulge</h1><div class=\"meta\">1944-12-16 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Germany launches its last major offensive on the Western Front.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1944-12-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-01-12-068":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0068 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Vistula–Oder Offensive</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-01-12 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces begin a broad offensive on the road to Berlin.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-01-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-01-16-ind-071":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-071 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Renault Nationalised</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-01-16 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The state restructures one of France&#x27;s major automobile manufacturers after the occupation.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 16 January 1945, in France, the milestone “Renault Nationalised” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The state restructures one of France&#x27;s major automobile manufacturers after the occupation. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry / post-war economy milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/loda/id/JORFTEXT000000886347\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-01-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-01-20-ind-072":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-072 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First K-25 Production Cell Becomes Operational</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-01-20 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The full arrangement had 54 main production units; all were operating by 15 August 1945.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 20 January 1945, in United States, the milestone “First K-25 Production Cell Becomes Operational” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The full arrangement had 54 main production units; all were operating by 15 August 1945. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://k25atomichistorycenter.org/timeline/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-01-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-01-27-069":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0069 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Liberation of Auschwitz</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-01-27 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Red Army enters the Auschwitz concentration and extermination camp complex.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-01-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-02-04-070":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0070 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Yalta Conference</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-02-04 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The leaders of the Soviet Union, the United States and Britain discuss the end of the war and post-war Europe.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-02-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-02-05-ind-073":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-073 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Hanford Sends Its First Shipment of Plutonium to Los Alamos</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-02-05 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The new industrial cycle from reactor production to chemical separation is now functioning in practice.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 5 February 1945, in United States, the milestone “Hanford Sends Its First Shipment of Plutonium to Los Alamos” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The new industrial cycle from reactor production to chemical separation is now functioning in practice. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry / logistics milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>The industrial achievement formed part of the atomic-weapons programme. Its engineering scale is historically important, but so are the programme’s military purpose and consequences.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/mapr/learn/photosmultimedia/hanford-t-plant-virtual-tour.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-02-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-02-13-116":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0116 · Air War</div><h1>Bombing of Dresden</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-02-13 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">British and American aircraft carry out mass air raids on the German city of Dresden.</p><p>In February 1945, Allied strategic bombers attacked the city in a series of raids that caused major fires. Thousands of civilians and refugees were among the dead.</p><p>The scale of the destruction and the number of victims became the subject of prolonged research and political propaganda. Historians study the raids in the wider context of the strategic air war.</p><p>Dresden became one of the best-known examples in debates about military necessity, proportionality and the humanitarian consequences of bombing cities.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1945)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-02-13\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-02-19-071":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0071 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of Iwo Jima</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-02-19 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American forces land on the Japanese island.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-02-19\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-03-07-072":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0072 · Western Europe</div><h1>Crossing of the Rhine at Remagen</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-03-07 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American forces capture a bridge across the Rhine.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-03-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-03-09-117":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0117 · Air War</div><h1>Firebombing of Tokyo</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-03-09 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">During the night of 9–10 March, American bombers carry out a devastating incendiary attack on Tokyo.</p><p>The mass B-29 raid caused fires in densely built urban districts. Flames spread rapidly and many people were unable to escape from the burning neighbourhoods.</p><p>The attack killed an enormous number of civilians and destroyed large areas of the city. The strategic bombing campaign against Japanese cities continued afterwards.</p><p>The event became one of the greatest humanitarian disasters of the air war and a major part of debates over its moral limits.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1945)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-03-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-04-01-073":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0073 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of Okinawa</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-04-01 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American landings on Okinawa begin a prolonged battle.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-04-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-04-12-118":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0118 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Death of Franklin D. Roosevelt</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-04-12 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The US president dies during the final campaigns of the Second World War.</p><p>Roosevelt had led the United States through its entry into the war and the formation of the anti-Hitler coalition. His death was an unexpected political shock for the Allies.</p><p>Presidential powers passed to Harry Truman, who had to master complex questions of European settlement and the war against Japan very quickly.</p><p>The change in US leadership occurred on the eve of the founding of the United Nations, Germany&#x27;s surrender and major decisions on how to end the Pacific War.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1945)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-04-12\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-04-16-074":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0074 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Berlin Strategic Offensive Operation</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-04-16 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Soviet forces begin the final offensive on the capital of Nazi Germany.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-04-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-04-25-075":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0075 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Meeting on the Elbe</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-04-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">American and Soviet troops meet near Torgau.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-04-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-04-25-new-120":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-120 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>San Francisco Conference Opens</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-04-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Delegates begin discussing the Charter of the United Nations.</p><p>Delegates begin discussing the Charter of the United Nations.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v01/d179\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-04-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-04-30-076":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0076 · Eastern Europe and the USSR</div><h1>Suicide of Adolf Hitler</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-04-30 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Adolf Hitler dies in Berlin as the Nazi regime collapses.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-04-30\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-05-07-new011":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0211 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>German Surrender Signed at Reims</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-05-07 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">By spring 1945, German forces were losing control across Europe. The leadership of the Third Reich could no longer continue the war on two fronts.</p><p>By spring 1945, German forces were losing control across Europe. The leadership of the Third Reich could no longer continue the war on two fronts.</p><p>On 7 May 1945, representatives of the German command signed an act of unconditional surrender to the Allied forces at Reims.</p><p>The document provided for the cessation of hostilities the following day. A separate signing ceremony of a similar instrument later took place in Berlin.</p><p>The events of 7 and 8 May should be distinguished: the Reims signing, the entry into force of the surrender, and the Berlin signing occurred at different times and had different political contexts.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-05-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-05-08-077":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0077 · Western Europe</div><h1>German Surrender Takes Effect</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-05-08 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The act of unconditional surrender takes effect on 8 May in Central European time, corresponding to 9 May in Moscow time.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-and-world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-05-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-06-22-078":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0078 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Battle of Okinawa Ends</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-06-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Organised Japanese resistance on Okinawa comes to an end.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-06-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-06-22-ind-074":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-074 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the Vickers Viking Passenger Aircraft</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-06-22 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of Britain&#x27;s first new passenger aircraft of the post-war era.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 22 June 1945, in United Kingdom, the milestone “First Flight of the Vickers Viking Passenger Aircraft” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: One of Britain&#x27;s first new passenger aircraft of the post-war era. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This civil aviation milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/research-enquiries/history-of-aviation-timeline/british-civil-aviation/1945-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-06-22\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-06-26-119":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0119 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>UN Charter Signed</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-06-26 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Delegates at the San Francisco Conference sign the Charter of the United Nations.</p><p>The experience of two world wars encouraged the Allies to seek a new system of collective security. Delegates negotiated the powers of UN institutions and principles of international cooperation.</p><p>The Charter established goals including the maintenance of peace, the development of friendly relations and the protection of human rights. It required ratification before the organisation could formally begin operating.</p><p>The Charter entered into force on 24 October 1945, creating an institution that became the central forum of world diplomacy.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1945)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-06-26\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-06-28-ind-075":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-075 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Willow Run Produces Its Final B-24</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-06-28 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The plant built 8,685 B-24 bombers.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 28 June 1945, in United States, the milestone “Willow Run Produces Its Final B-24” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The plant built 8,685 B-24 bombers. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft manufacturing / mass production milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/artifact/199261\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-06-28\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-07-16-120":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0120 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Trinity Nuclear Test</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-07-16 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The United States conducts the world&#x27;s first detonation of a nuclear device in New Mexico.</p><p>The test was the culmination of work by the Manhattan Project, whose scientists and engineers operated under strict secrecy. It demonstrated the practical feasibility of a new type of weapon.</p><p>The successful test had direct significance for the military and political decisions of the US leadership during the final weeks of the war with Japan.</p><p>Trinity marked the beginning of the nuclear age, whose consequences shaped international security and public debate for decades.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1945)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-07-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-07-17-079":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0079 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Potsdam Conference</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-07-17 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Allied leaders discuss the post-war settlement.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-07-17\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-08-06-080":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0080 · Air War</div><h1>Atomic Bombing of Hiroshima</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-08-06 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The United States drops an atomic bomb on the Japanese city, killing large numbers of civilians.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-08-06\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-08-08-081":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0081 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Soviet Union Enters the War against Japan</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-08-08 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Soviet Union declares war on Japan; the offensive in Manchuria begins on 9 August.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-08-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-08-09-082":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0082 · Air War</div><h1>Atomic Bombing of Nagasaki</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-08-09 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A second American atomic bomb strikes the Japanese city.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-08-09\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-08-14-new012":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0212 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Japan Agrees to Surrender</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-08-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">In August 1945, Japan faced the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki and the Soviet entry into the war in the Far East.</p><p>In August 1945, Japan faced the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki and the Soviet entry into the war in the Far East.</p><p>On 14 August, Japan&#x27;s leadership agreed to accept the terms of surrender. The emperor&#x27;s decision was announced publicly in Japan on 15 August.</p><p>The announcement that the war would end was not the same as signing the formal instrument, which took place on 2 September 1945.</p><p>The decision completed the political process of Japan&#x27;s withdrawal from the war and opened the way to Allied occupation and post-war reforms.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-08-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-08-15-083":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0083 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Japan Announces Surrender</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-08-15 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Emperor of Japan informs the population that the surrender terms have been accepted.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-08-15\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-08-17-121":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0121 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Indonesian Declaration of Independence</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-08-17 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Sukarno and Mohammad Hatta proclaim Indonesian independence after Japan&#x27;s surrender.</p><p>The Japanese occupation had dismantled the former system of Dutch colonial control. When Japan agreed to surrender, Indonesian leaders quickly used the political opening.</p><p>The proclamation did not bring immediate international recognition. Years of armed conflict, diplomacy and negotiations with the Netherlands followed.</p><p>The event illustrates how the end of the world war accelerated the disintegration of colonial empires in Southeast Asia.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II_(1945)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-08-17\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-09-02-084":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0084 · Asia and the Pacific</div><h1>Formal Surrender of Japan</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-09-02 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The surrender instrument is signed aboard USS Missouri, bringing the Second World War to a formal end.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-the-pacific\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-09-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-09-05-ind-077":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-077 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>ZEEP Reactor Reaches Criticality for the First Time</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-09-05 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The first nuclear reactor outside the United States to achieve a self-sustaining chain reaction.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 5 September 1945, in Canada, the milestone “ZEEP Reactor Reaches Criticality for the First Time” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The first nuclear reactor outside the United States to achieve a self-sustaining chain reaction. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear energy / science milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://nrc.canada.ca/en/corporate/history/1946-1964\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-09-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-09-20-ind-078":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-078 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Rolls-Royce Trent Makes Its First Flight as an Early Turboprop Engine</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-09-20 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A key early milestone in turboprop transport aviation.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 20 September 1945, in United Kingdom, the milestone “Rolls-Royce Trent Makes Its First Flight as an Early Turboprop Engine” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A key early milestone in turboprop transport aviation. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This aircraft engine manufacturing milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/research-enquiries/history-of-aviation-timeline/british-civil-aviation/1945-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-09-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-09-25-ind-079":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-079 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Flight of the de Havilland Dove</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-09-25 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A symbol of aircraft factories shifting toward post-war civil production.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 25 September 1945, in United Kingdom, the milestone “First Flight of the de Havilland Dove” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A symbol of aircraft factories shifting toward post-war civil production. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This civil aviation milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/archive-exhibitions/de-havilland-the-man-and-the-company/photographs/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-09-25\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","world-1945-10-24-04":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №1004 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>UN Charter Enters into Force</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-10-24 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Charter of the United Nations, signed in San Francisco on 26 June 1945, enters into force on 24 October, marking the formal beginning of the United Nations.</p><p>The Charter of the United Nations, signed in San Francisco on 26 June 1945, entered into force on 24 October. The date is regarded as the formal beginning of the United Nations.</p><p>The organisation was intended to create a system of international cooperation and mechanisms for maintaining peace after the global conflict. Signing the Charter and its entry into force are two different dates.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.un.org/en/about-us/un-charter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-10-24\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-10-24-085":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0085 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>United Nations Formally Established</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-10-24 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The Charter of the United Nations enters into force.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-10-24\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-11-16-worldnew005":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew005 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>UNESCO Constitution Adopted</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-11-16 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">States agree on the founding principles of an international organisation for cooperation in education, science and culture.</p><p>On 16 November 1945 in London, the Constitution of the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization — UNESCO — was adopted. Its creation formed part of post-war efforts to build international mechanisms of cooperation.</p><p>The founding document stressed that lasting peace required not only political agreements but also mutual understanding, education and the intellectual solidarity of humankind. This reflected the attempt to draw lessons from the catastrophe of world war.</p><p>The Constitution defined the future organisation&#x27;s areas of work and mechanisms for state participation. Adoption of the text did not mean that it entered into force immediately; the required number of acceptances had first to be deposited.</p><p>UNESCO gradually became an important forum for international cooperation in heritage, science and education. The event shows how the post-war world also tried to prevent future conflict through cultural means.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/constitution\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-11-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-11-20-086":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0086 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Nuremberg Trials Begin</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-11-20 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The International Military Tribunal opens proceedings against major Nazi leaders.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-11-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1945-12-27-ind-080":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-080 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Series Production of Volkswagen Resumes at Wolfsburg</h1><div class=\"meta\">1945-12-27 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">55 cars were produced by the end of 1945.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 27 December 1945, in Germany, the milestone “Series Production of Volkswagen Resumes at Wolfsburg” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: 55 cars were produced by the end of 1945. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry / post-war reconstruction milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>This industrial history is inseparable from the Nazi war economy. The related article must also address coercion and forced labour where documented; the technical achievement is not presented as a value-neutral success.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.volkswagen-newsroom.com/en/press-releases/75-years-ago-in-wolfsburg-start-of-series-production-of-the-volkswagen-beetle-6732\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1945-12-27\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-01-10-087":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0087 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>First UN General Assembly</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-01-10 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The first session of the United Nations General Assembly opens in London.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-01-10\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-01-24-new-123":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-123 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>UN General Assembly Creates an Atomic Energy Commission</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-01-24 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The General Assembly adopts its first resolution, addressing atomic energy.</p><p>The General Assembly adopts its first resolution, addressing atomic energy.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/671200\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-01-24\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","world-1946-02-14-05":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №1005 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>ENIAC Publicly Unveiled</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-02-14 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The United States unveils ENIAC, a large-scale electronic digital computer whose calculation speed greatly exceeded that of most contemporary electromechanical systems.</p><p>The United States publicly unveiled ENIAC, a large-scale electronic digital computer. Its computing speed was far higher than that of most contemporary electromechanical systems.</p><p>This public presentation on 14 February should not be confused with the machine&#x27;s formal dedication ceremony on 16 February 1946. ENIAC became an important milestone in the transition to electronic computing.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.computerhistory.org/tdih/February/14/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-02-14\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-03-05-122":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0122 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Churchill&#x27;s Fulton Speech</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-03-05 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Winston Churchill delivers his &#x27;Iron Curtain&#x27; speech about the division of Europe.</p><p>After the Allies&#x27; common victory, disagreements about Europe&#x27;s future grew rapidly. Churchill drew attention to the spread of Soviet political control in Eastern Europe.</p><p>The speech was not the formal beginning of the Cold War, but it became one of its best-known early symbols. Reactions in different countries were mixed.</p><p>The Fulton speech illustrates the changing political atmosphere after 1945: former allies increasingly perceived each other as rivals.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_timelines_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-03-05\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-03-08-ind-081":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-081 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Bell 47 Receives the First Civil Helicopter Certification</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-03-08 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">It opened the civilian production market for helicopters.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 8 March 1946, in United States, the milestone “Bell 47 Receives the First Civil Helicopter Certification” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: It opened the civilian production market for helicopters. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This civil aviation / engineering milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.si.edu/object/nasm_A19650240000\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-03-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-04-08-ind-082":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-082 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Électricité de France (EDF) Created</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-04-08 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">More than 1,650 electricity and gas companies were consolidated.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 8 April 1946, in France, the milestone “Électricité de France (EDF) Created” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: More than 1,650 electricity and gas companies were consolidated. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This energy / national infrastructure milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.edf.fr/en/the-edf-group/the-edf-group/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-04-08\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-04-23-ind-083":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-083 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Piaggio Registers the Vespa Industrial Model</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-04-23 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">2,484 scooters were produced by the end of 1946.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 23 April 1946, in Italy, the milestone “Piaggio Registers the Vespa Industrial Model” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: 2,484 scooters were produced by the end of 1946. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This mechanical engineering / mass mobility milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date, core fact and quantitative figure verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://leonardo.museopiaggio.it/it/collezioni/1-vespa/3-98-mp6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-04-23\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-05-03-088":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0088 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Tokyo War Crimes Tribunal Begins</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-05-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Proceedings begin against senior Japanese military and political leaders.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-05-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-05-07-ind-084":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-084 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Tokyo Tsushin Kogyo — the Future Sony — Founded</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-05-07 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The beginning of one of the emblematic companies of Japan&#x27;s high-technology industry.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 7 May 1946, in Japan, the milestone “Tokyo Tsushin Kogyo — the Future Sony — Founded” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The beginning of one of the emblematic companies of Japan&#x27;s high-technology industry. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This electronics milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Year and core fact verified; no unsupported day assigned. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.sony.com/en/SonyInfo/CorporateInfo/History/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-05-07\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-06-17-ind-085":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-085 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Commercial Mobile Telephone Service Launched</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-06-17 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Practical mobile telephony decades before cellular networks.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 17 June 1946, in United States, the milestone “First Commercial Mobile Telephone Service Launched” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Practical mobile telephony decades before cellular networks. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This telecommunications milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.att.com/blog/history-of-the-mobile-phone-from-1g-to-5g/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-06-17\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-07-04-089":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0089 · Holocaust and Civilians</div><h1>Kielce Pogrom</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-07-04 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Anti-Jewish violence in post-war Poland kills dozens of people.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline/holocaust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-07-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-08-01-ind-087":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-087 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Atomic Energy Act of 1946 Signed</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-08-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The US atomic complex shifts from wartime to civilian federal administration.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 August 1946, in United States, the milestone “Atomic Energy Act of 1946 Signed” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: The US atomic complex shifts from wartime to civilian federal administration. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry / public administration milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.energy.gov/management/august-1-1946-atomic-energy-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-08-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-08-02-ind-088":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-088 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Official Shipment of Reactor-Produced Radioisotopes Sent for Civilian Use</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-08-02 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Reactor technology begins moving into medicine, biology and industrial research.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 2 August 1946, in United States, the milestone “First Official Shipment of Reactor-Produced Radioisotopes Sent for Civilian Use” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: Reactor technology begins moving into medicine, biology and industrial research. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This nuclear industry / medicine milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ornl.gov/index.php/node/111548\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-08-02\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-09-01-ind-089":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-089 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Vickers Viking Enters Regular Service with British European Airways</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-09-01 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">A new production passenger aircraft for rebuilding Europe&#x27;s air network.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 1 September 1946, in United Kingdom, the milestone “Vickers Viking Enters Regular Service with British European Airways” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: A new production passenger aircraft for rebuilding Europe&#x27;s air network. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This civil aviation milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/research-enquiries/history-of-aviation-timeline/british-civil-aviation/1946-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-09-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-09-20-worldnew006":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew006 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>First Cannes Film Festival Opens</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-09-20 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the wartime interruption, Cannes hosts an international programme of screenings and cultural events.</p><p>On 20 September 1946, the first Cannes Film Festival actually held opened. Its organisers wanted to create an international forum for cinema after years of war and broken cultural contacts.</p><p>The programme included films from several countries and events devoted to the history of cinema. The festival&#x27;s official archive preserves a schedule beginning on 20 September 1946.</p><p>The festival had been planned before the Second World War, but the conflict prevented the project from proceeding at the intended time. The 1946 event therefore became a symbol of the return of international cultural life.</p><p>Cannes later developed into one of the world&#x27;s most influential film festivals. Its history illustrates how cultural institutions were rebuilt in post-war Europe.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.festival-cannes.com/en/2019/the-1946-festival-de-cannes-programme/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-09-20\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-10-01-090":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0090 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Nuremberg Tribunal Judgments</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-10-01 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Judgments are delivered against the principal defendants in the international trial.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline/holocaust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-10-01\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-10-03-ind-090":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №IND-090 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>Renault 4CV Publicly Unveiled at the Reopened Paris Motor Show</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-10-03 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">One of the symbols of post-war mass motorisation in Europe.</p><p>Industry, infrastructure and technology changed rapidly between 1938 and 1946, reshaping production, mobility and the material foundations of war and post-war recovery.</p><p>On 3 October 1946, in France, the milestone “Renault 4CV Publicly Unveiled at the Reopened Paris Motor Show” occurred. WWII CHRONOS records it as a specific industrial or technological event rather than as a vague multi-year trend.</p><p>Verified figure or fact: One of the symbols of post-war mass motorisation in Europe. Physical measurements in WWII CHRONOS are expressed in the metric system.</p><p>This automotive industry / mass market milestone helps explain the material systems behind the era: factories, energy, transport networks, extraction, communications, new materials and production methods.</p><p>Where an industrial achievement was tied to war, coercion or forced labour, the technical accomplishment must be read together with that human and political context rather than celebrated in isolation.</p><p>Editorial status: Date and core fact verified. The source below supports the period and central claim; unsupported details have deliberately not been added.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.renaultgroup.com/en/magazine/our-group-news/renault-group-and-the-paris-motor-show-two-stories-one-shared-history-1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-10-03\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-10-16-123":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №0123 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>Executions of Those Sentenced at Nuremberg</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-10-16 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">Some of the Nazi leaders sentenced to death by the International Military Tribunal are executed.</p><p>The judgments were delivered on 1 October 1946 after a lengthy trial examining documents, orders and testimony about crimes committed by the Nazi regime.</p><p>The death sentences were carried out on 16 October. Other defendants received prison sentences or were acquitted.</p><p>The Nuremberg Trial established the principle that state leaders can bear individual criminal responsibility for international crimes.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_timelines_of_World_War_II\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-10-16\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-11-04-worldnew007":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №worldnew007 · World Events and Discoveries</div><h1>UNESCO Constitution Enters into Force</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-11-04 · KEY EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">After the required acceptances, UNESCO&#x27;s founding document acquires legal force.</p><p>On 4 November 1946, the UNESCO Constitution entered into force after the conditions for acceptance by member states had been fulfilled. This was a separate event from the adoption of the document in London on 16 November 1945.</p><p>With the Constitution in force, the international organisation received a formal legal basis for work in education, science, culture and the exchange of knowledge. It became part of the broader post-war system of international cooperation.</p><p>The founders argued that ignorance, prejudice and mutual distrust contributed to conflict, and that durable peace therefore required institutions supporting cultural and educational connections.</p><p>The distinction between adoption and entry into force is important for historical accuracy: the first is agreement on the text, the second marks the legal beginning of the founding document&#x27;s operation.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://unesdoc.unesco.org/in/rest/annotationSVC/DownloadAttachment/attach_cmsUpload_8c5f1a34-ecad-42f1-a02a-c1b470ceece7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-11-04\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>","1946-12-11-new-126":"<div class=\"kicker\">ARCHIVE FILE №NEW-126 · Diplomacy and the Post-war World</div><h1>UNICEF Established</h1><div class=\"meta\">1946-12-11 · ARCHIVE EVENT</div><p class=\"lead\">The UN General Assembly creates the children&#x27;s fund.</p><p>The UN General Assembly creates the children&#x27;s fund.</p><p>A detailed historical account of this event is being prepared. An independent source for further study is provided below.</p><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.un.org/en/ccoi/unicef-united-nations-childrens-fund\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View historical source ↗</a></p><div class=\"article-nav\"><a href=\"./?date=1946-12-11\">← RETURN TO THE WEEK</a></div><div class=\"adslot\">AD BLOCK · INACTIVE</div>"};
